# 13. Implementation roadmap

## First 30 days: Establish control

- Build the SKU-customer-channel pocket-margin waterfall;
- identify the largest sources of the six-point decline;
- freeze unplanned promotions and discretionary leakage;
- review the three delisted products;
- classify SKUs by strategic role;
- identify known-value items and affordability thresholds;
- quantify low-margin customers, SKUs, and service patterns;
- create a cross-functional margin-recovery council;
- set account-specific negotiation floors and approval rules.

## Days 30–90: Design and negotiate

- Estimate elasticity and cross-elasticity for priority SKUs;
- design selective price actions;
- build good-better-best pack ladders;
- identify tail-SKU rationalization;
- redesign the next two promotional cycles;
- validate initial formulation and packaging savings;
- prepare retailer-specific category-profit stories;
- present retailers with phased option menus;
- negotiate give-gets and performance reviews.

Focus first on combinations with strong economics and low distribution risk.

## Months 3–6: Pilot

- Test selective pricing by market, account, or SKU where feasible;
- launch one or two entry-price and premium pack changes;
- implement performance-based promotional pilots;
- test channel-specific formats;
- pilot cost-engineered formulations or packaging after full validation;
- implement logistics term changes with selected customers;
- measure consumer switching, retailer profit, and distribution weekly.

Use control groups where possible and define rollback triggers before launch.

## Months 6–12: Scale

- Expand successful price and pack moves;
- remove or redesign structurally weak SKUs;
- roll out promotion optimization;
- scale procurement, yield, energy, and freight initiatives;
- introduce account-level price and trade-spend governance;
- embed retailer and consumer elasticity into planning;
- align sales incentives to profitable net revenue rather than gross shipments.

## Months 12–18: Institutionalize

- Refresh price-pack architecture at least annually;
- incorporate inflation scenarios into innovation and sourcing;
- establish commodity and freight review mechanisms;
- embed margin thresholds into new-product development;
- use assortment and promotion optimization continuously;
- renegotiate structurally uneconomic customer terms;
- maintain affordable products while funding premium innovation.

---

# Final recommendation

The producer should recover margin through **precision rather than another blunt price round**.

The plan should:

1. Protect known-value products and price-sensitive consumers.
2. Take selective pricing on differentiated, less elastic SKUs.
3. Preserve accessible shelf prices with economically designed entry packs.
4. create core-value and premium tiers that encourage profitable trade-up.
5. Tailor packs and terms to each channel’s shopper mission and cost to serve.
6. Eliminate nonincremental promotions while preserving strategically important events.
7. Rationalize low-volume complexity and direct demand toward higher-contribution products.
8. Engineer cost out of ingredients, packaging, production, and freight without damaging consumer-relevant quality.
9. Negotiate retailer-specific menus supported by category-profit and shopper economics.
10. Exchange concessions for distribution, execution, logistics efficiency, or measurable performance.
11. Phase and test major changes to avoid another distribution shock.
12. Measure gross profit dollars after trade spending, not price realization or margin percentage in isolation.

The central shift is from **cost-plus pricing** to integrated revenue and margin management. Sustained inflation cannot be solved solely through consumer price, and retailer acceptance cannot be won solely by presenting cost indices. The durable solution is a portfolio in which price points, pack sizes, promotion, assortment, product design, and customer terms work together to restore margin while protecting affordability, distribution, and retailer category economics.

# Monetization strategy: increase revenue per user without sacrificing trust

The app should not respond to sub-2% paid conversion with a harder paywall or a larger advertising load. The prior tests have already shown that the free user base is sensitive to abrupt changes in access and interruption. The more promising path is to monetize **different forms of value differently**:

- Subscription for recurring, high-frequency value;
- Usage-based pricing for episodic or compute-intensive value;
- Light, opt-in advertising for users who prefer free access;
- Partnerships for users whose value is better captured through commerce, distribution, or embedded use cases.

The central design principle should be:

> **Do not charge everyone more. Charge the users who receive, create, or require materially more value—and preserve a credible free experience for everyone else.**

The immediate objective should be to increase revenue per active user while keeping the free base healthy enough to support future conversion, referrals, data quality, and partner value.

---

## 1. Set the economic objectives correctly

A single paid-conversion target would be too narrow. Management should track the full monetization system:

### Primary targets

Over 12–18 months:

- Increase blended revenue per monthly active user by **2–3 times**;
- Increase paid conversion from below 2% to approximately **4–6%**, without requiring every segment to convert;
- Maintain or improve 90-day retention for free users;
- Keep paid-user retention and renewal economics healthy;
- Reduce dependence on any single monetization source;
- Reach a credible path to contribution break-even, including infrastructure, customer support, payment fees, and partner revenue share.

A useful revenue bridge is:

\[
\text{Revenue per MAU}
=
(\text{paid conversion}\times\text{paid ARPU})
+
(\text{ad-eligible users}\times\text{ad ARPU})
+
\text{usage revenue}
+
(\text{partner revenue})
\]

The dashboard should distinguish:

1. **Revenue per registered user**;
2. **Revenue per monthly active user**;
3. **Revenue per retained user**;
4. **Contribution per user**, after infrastructure, payment, support, and partner costs.

A pricing change that increases monthly revenue but shortens user lifetime may not be progress.

---

# 2. Segment users by value and behavior, not only demographics

The observed variation in usage suggests that a single premium tier is unlikely to capture the opportunity. The first step should be a behavioral segmentation based on the last 60–90 days of activity.

## Recommended initial segments

| Segment | Typical behavior | Value signal | Likely monetization |
|---|---|---|---|
| Casual explorers | Infrequent use, low feature depth | Uses core function occasionally | Free tier, light ads, partner offers |
| Habitual free users | Frequent use but limited premium behavior | Strong engagement and retention | Low-cost subscription or sponsored access |
| Power users | High frequency, advanced features, high session depth | Product is part of a routine or workflow | Premium subscription, usage add-ons |
| Professional or commercial users | Usage linked to work, output, or revenue | Clear economic benefit | Higher-priced pro tier, usage pricing, team plans |
| Episodic intensive users | Low average frequency but occasional spikes | High value during projects or events | Credits, passes, pay-as-you-go |
| At-risk or declining users | Previously active, now reducing usage | Retention risk rather than immediate monetization | No hard sell; reactivation and value recovery |
| New users | Limited history | Uncertain long-term value | Guided onboarding and delayed monetization |
| Partner-eligible users | Behavior aligns with a relevant service or category | Potential external commercial value | Carefully selected partnership offers |

The segment assignment should remain dynamic. A user can move from casual to habitual, or from free to professional, based on behavior. Monetization should follow observed value rather than forcing a permanent label.

### Important distinction

A user who opens the app frequently is not necessarily willing to pay. The strongest purchase signals are likely to be:

- Repeated use of advanced features;
- Reaching a free usage limit;
- Returning to the same workflow;
- Saving, exporting, sharing, or collaborating;
- Using the product in a time-sensitive or professional context;
- Showing frustration with limitations;
- Attempting to access a premium feature more than once.

These events should drive offers more reliably than session count alone.

---

# 3. Use a four-part monetization portfolio

## A. Subscription tiers: monetize recurring value

Subscriptions should become more relevant, but not through a universal premium wall. The app should offer a clear ladder.

### Free

Purpose: maintain reach, habit formation, referrals, and a credible product experience.

Include:

- Core functionality;
- A reasonable monthly usage allowance;
- Basic history or storage;
- Non-intrusive contextual advertising, where appropriate;
- Access to selected community or partner benefits;
- No forced interstitial ad during a core task.

The free tier must feel intentionally designed, not like a broken version of the paid product.

### Plus or Personal

Purpose: convert habitual users who value convenience but are not professionals.

Potential benefits:

- Ad-free experience;
- Higher usage allowance;
- Faster processing or response;
- More history, storage, or customization;
- Access to a defined set of premium features;
- Better export, sync, or personalization.

This should be priced as an approachable upgrade rather than a professional commitment. A monthly option is important, but an annual plan can be presented after the user has demonstrated sustained value.

### Pro

Purpose: monetize power users and users with clear economic benefit.

Potential benefits:

- Substantially higher or unlimited usage, subject to fair-use controls;
- Advanced tools and controls;
- Priority speed or reliability;
- Larger exports, automation, integrations, or collaboration;
- Commercial-use rights;
- Better support;
- Team or workspace functionality.

The Pro tier should not merely be “more of everything.” It should make the professional use case visibly easier or more productive.

### Team or Business

Purpose: capture organizations currently using individual accounts or sharing informal workflows.

Potential benefits:

- Shared workspaces;
- Administration and permissions;
- Centralized billing;
- Usage visibility;
- Collaboration and audit functions;
- Service-level commitments;
- Integration and support.

This tier may produce more revenue with fewer users and should be tested once product usage shows repeated collaboration or commercial activity.

### A possible packaging structure

| Tier | Primary user | Economic role |
|---|---|---|
| Free | Casual and uncertain users | Reach and future conversion |
| Plus | Habitual personal users | Broad conversion |
| Pro | Power and professional users | ARPU expansion |
| Team | Commercial and collaborative users | High-value account revenue |

The product should avoid introducing too many tiers at once. A sensible first release is Free, Plus, and Pro, with Team piloted through an assisted or invitation-based program.

---

## B. Usage-based pricing: capture episodic intensity

A subscription is poorly suited to users whose value is concentrated in occasional periods. For those users, usage-based pricing can be more acceptable than forcing a recurring commitment.

Possible structures include:

- Credit packs;
- One-week or one-month passes;
- Pay-per-export;
- Pay-per-advanced action;
- Project bundles;
- Usage above a subscription allowance;
- One-time upgrades for a defined task.

The charge should be attached to a resource the user can understand. “Credits” are workable only if the conversion is transparent. Users should be able to see:

- What one credit purchases;
- How many credits remain;
- When credits expire, if they expire;
- Whether unused credits roll over;
- The expected cost before confirming a charge.

### Best-fit users

- Episodic intensive users;
- Free users who repeatedly hit a limit but do not want a subscription;
- Professional users with unpredictable demand;
- Users conducting a one-time project or event;
- Former subscribers who still have occasional high-value needs.

### Guard against bill shock

Usage pricing should include:

- Pre-purchase confirmation;
- Spending caps;
- Notifications at 50%, 80%, and 100% of a limit;
- Optional automatic top-up, disabled by default;
- A clear monthly ceiling;
- Refund or goodwill policies for unclear first-time charges.

It may be useful to offer a subscription with included credits and then sell additional credits. This creates a bridge between predictable subscription revenue and intensive usage.

---

## C. Advertising: keep it contextual, limited, and optional where possible

The previous ad test damaged ratings quickly. That is a strong indication that the problem was not necessarily advertising itself, but the **placement, frequency, or interruption model**.

The app should avoid:

- Full-screen ads during core tasks;
- Ads immediately after opening the app;
- Repeated ads within a short session;
- Ads that obscure content or delay completion;
- Aggressive ad personalization without clear consent;
- Reward systems that make ordinary use feel artificially restricted.

### More defensible advertising formats

1. **Native, contextual placements**  
   Ads appear in natural browsing or discovery surfaces rather than during task completion.

2. **Sponsored tools or templates**  
   A partner sponsors a useful feature, workflow, content set, or resource.

3. **Opt-in rewarded access**  
   A user may choose to view an ad in exchange for a clearly stated benefit, such as a temporary usage extension. The core experience should remain available without watching.

4. **Partner marketplace offers**  
   Relevant services are surfaced at an appropriate point in the user journey, with clear labeling.

5. **Email or notification sponsorships**  
   Used cautiously and only with consent. These should not become a substitute for a damaged in-app experience.

### Ad guardrails

- Set a frequency cap by user and day;
- Exclude new users until they have established value;
- Exclude users in sensitive or high-friction workflows;
- Suppress ads for users who have recently experienced an error or failed task;
- Measure ad revenue against session abandonment, not only impressions;
- Remove placements that increase negative ratings or support complaints.

For the most valuable free users, an ad-free Plus tier may be more economically attractive than maximizing ad impressions.

---

## D. Partnerships: monetize distribution and use cases

Partnerships can improve revenue without charging the user directly, but only where the partner genuinely adds value. Poorly matched offers can create the impression that the app is selling user attention rather than serving users.

Potential partnership models include:

- Sponsored access through employers, schools, associations, or platforms;
- Bundling the app with another subscription;
- Affiliate revenue from relevant services;
- Embedded or white-label versions;
- API licensing;
- Enterprise distribution;
- Co-branded workflows;
- Partner-funded credits for eligible users;
- Referral fees for complementary products.

### Best-fit users

- Professional users who already operate in a partner ecosystem;
- Students, employees, or members of an institution;
- Users who repeatedly export or integrate their work;
- Users with activity patterns connected to a complementary service;
- Regions or segments where direct payment conversion is structurally difficult.

Partnerships should be evaluated on contribution, not headline contract value:

\[
\text{Partner contribution}
=
\text{partner revenue}
-
\text{revenue share}
-
\text{support cost}
-
\text{integration cost}
-
\text{incremental infrastructure}
\]

The company should be cautious about exclusive partnerships that limit future pricing flexibility or compromise user trust.

---

# 4. Map offers to user segments

The offers should appear when the user has demonstrated the relevant need, not simply because a calendar event or marketing campaign has arrived.

| Segment | Primary offer | Secondary offer | What to avoid |
|---|---|---|---|
| Casual explorers | Free tier with low-frequency contextual ads | One-time pass for an intensive need | Early paywall or subscription pressure |
| Habitual free users | Plus tier with ad removal and convenience benefits | Opt-in rewarded usage or annual plan | Charging for the basic habit |
| Power users | Pro subscription | Additional usage credits or annual commitment | Unlimited usage without cost controls |
| Professional/commercial | Pro, Team, or Business | Usage-based overage and API/integration pricing | Consumer pricing that hides commercial value |
| Episodic intensive | Credits, project pass, or short-term subscription | Upgrade path to Plus or Pro | Annual subscription as the only option |
| At-risk users | Product education, recovery, and reactivation | Targeted temporary benefit | Immediate price increase or hard limit |
| New users | Free onboarding and feature discovery | Delayed, behavior-triggered trial | Monetization before value is understood |
| Partner-eligible | Sponsored access, bundle, or relevant offer | Direct Plus or Pro conversion | Irrelevant affiliate advertising |

### Trigger-based offer examples

- A user reaches the free allowance for the first time: show a transparent choice between waiting, buying credits, or starting a trial.
- A user repeatedly reaches the allowance across two or three periods: offer Plus or Pro, depending on feature depth.
- A user exports or shares commercially relevant work: introduce Pro or Team functionality.
- A user declines a subscription but returns for intensive usage: offer a project pass or credit pack.
- A user has declining activity: avoid monetization pressure and test reactivation instead.
- A user has been active for several weeks without using advanced features: show an education-led upgrade, not a discount-led upgrade.

---

# 5. Design the paywall as a graduated conversation

The earlier aggressive paywall likely converted some users while destroying more lifetime value than it created. The next model should be progressive.

## Stage 1: demonstrate value

The user receives a complete core experience. Premium features can be visible but should be explained through previews, examples, or limited trials.

## Stage 2: signal the limit before blocking

Before a user reaches a limit, show:

- Current usage;
- Remaining allowance;
- What happens next;
- Available alternatives;
- A simple explanation of the upgrade benefit.

## Stage 3: offer choice

At the limit, present multiple paths:

- Continue with the free allowance after reset;
- Watch an optional rewarded ad, if appropriate;
- Purchase a credit pack;
- Start Plus or Pro;
- Use a lower-cost alternative workflow.

The user should not feel trapped.

## Stage 4: learn from the choice

The response itself is valuable data:

- Subscription selected;
- Credit pack selected;
- Ad selected;
- Waited;
- Abandoned;
- Uninstalled;
- Contacted support.

The product team should treat each outcome as a pricing and packaging signal rather than optimizing only for immediate checkout conversion.

---

# 6. Experiment plan

A controlled experimentation program is needed, but the company should avoid testing multiple forms of friction simultaneously. The first goal is to identify monetization that does not impair the product’s underlying health.

## Phase 0: measurement and instrumentation — 2 to 4 weeks

Before further pricing tests:

- Define a consistent active-user and retained-user denominator;
- Instrument every paywall, offer impression, dismissal, purchase, refund, and cancellation;
- Record usage immediately before and after an offer;
- Link app-store ratings and support contacts to release versions and experiment cohorts;
- Establish segment-level baselines;
- Separate organic churn from monetization-induced churn where possible;
- Build a revenue and contribution cohort view by acquisition source.

The company should not make a broad monetization decision while the behavioral data remains blended across segments.

## Phase 1: packaging tests — 4 to 6 weeks

Test the offer structure before increasing friction:

1. Free versus Free plus Plus;
2. Plus benefits: ad-free, convenience, higher limits, or feature access;
3. Monthly versus annual presentation;
4. Credit packs versus short-term passes;
5. Pro packaging around advanced outcomes rather than raw limits.

Use randomized cohorts within each behavioral segment. Do not expose all users to every treatment.

## Phase 2: trigger and timing tests — 6 to 8 weeks

Test:

- Upgrade prompt after first limit versus repeated limit;
- Prompt after demonstrated premium behavior;
- In-product feature preview versus generic paywall;
- Trial length;
- Reminder timing;
- Offer at the end of a successful workflow versus during interruption.

A successful prompt should increase monetization without reducing the next-session return rate.

## Phase 3: advertising tests — 3 to 6 weeks

Test only low-intrusion formats:

- Native placement;
- Opt-in rewarded access;
- Frequency caps;
- Ad-free Plus positioning;
- No-ad control groups.

The ad test should be stopped early if ratings, task completion, or uninstall rates deteriorate beyond the approved threshold.

## Phase 4: partnership pilots — 8 to 12 weeks

Pilot one or two narrowly defined partnerships with:

- Clear user relevance;
- Simple attribution;
- Limited technical integration;
- A defined support model;
- No exclusivity initially;
- A measurable contribution target.

Partnerships should be tested by segment and geography, since willingness to pay and advertiser economics may vary substantially.

---

# 7. Define success using guardrails, not conversion alone

The company should use a balanced scorecard for every experiment.

## Core monetization metrics

- Paid conversion;
- Trial start and trial-to-paid conversion;
- Net revenue per MAU;
- Revenue per retained user;
- Subscription ARPU;
- Credit-pack purchase rate;
- Ad revenue per ad-eligible user;
- Partner revenue per eligible user;
- Gross contribution after variable costs;
- Refund and chargeback rate;
- Cancellation rate;
- Renewal rate;
- Upgrade and downgrade flows.

## Retention and product-health guardrails

Track these by segment and experiment cell:

- Day 1, Day 7, Day 30, and Day 90 retention;
- Weekly and monthly active-user rates;
- Session frequency;
- Core-task completion;
- Time to successful outcome;
- Usage of key features;
- Free-user reactivation;
- Paid-user renewal;
- Uninstall rate;
- Account deletion;
- Notification opt-out;
- Support contacts and complaint rate.

## Rating and trust metrics

- Average app-store rating;
- New rating velocity;
- Share of one- and two-star reviews;
- Review text mentioning ads, pricing, limits, or deception;
- “Not as described” or billing-related complaints;
- Refund requests;
- Chargebacks;
- Privacy or consent complaints.

Ratings should be monitored by app version and geography. A three-week delay in recognizing rating deterioration would be unnecessarily expensive.

## Early-warning thresholds

The following are reasonable initial controls, to be calibrated against baseline volatility:

- Pause an experiment if D30 retention falls more than **1.5 percentage points** versus control;
- Pause if uninstall rate increases more than **10% relative**;
- Pause if core-task completion declines more than **2%**;
- Pause if the share of negative reviews rises by **20% relative** or the average rating declines by **0.1 points**;
- Pause if support contacts related to billing or ads rise more than **15%**;
- Pause if paid cancellation within the first billing cycle exceeds control by **10% relative**;
- Pause if refunds or chargebacks increase materially;
- Require review if free-user engagement falls while monetization improves.

The thresholds should be enforced at segment level. A positive aggregate result can conceal severe harm among a large, low-monetization segment.

---

# 8. Use holdouts and long enough test windows

Short tests are particularly dangerous for subscription products. A paywall may look successful during the first week and then produce higher cancellations, lower referrals, and weaker organic acquisition.

Every major monetization change should include:

- A persistent control group;
- A holdout group retained after the experiment ends;
- At least one full billing cycle for subscription tests;
- Longer observation for app-store rating and uninstall effects;
- Segment-specific results;
- New-user and existing-user cohorts separated;
- Acquisition-source controls.

The decision metric should be incremental contribution over a defined period, not simply conversion at the point of offer.

For example:

\[
\text{Incremental contribution}
=
\text{monetization revenue}
-
\text{payment costs}
-
\text{support cost}
-
\text{incremental infrastructure}
-
\text{lost advertising}
-
\text{lost future user value}
\]

A treatment that raises paid conversion from 2% to 3.5% but causes a 15% decline in retained free users may not be attractive if those free users generate referrals, content, network value, or future conversion.

---

# 9. Recommended initial allocation by segment

The company can begin with an approximate monetization mix and refine it through testing:

| Segment | Free experience | Subscription | Usage pricing | Ads | Partnerships |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| Casual explorers | Strong | Low | Occasional | Light | Selective |
| Habitual free | Strong | Main conversion path | Limited | Reduced or removed in Plus | Moderate |
| Power users | Supported | Main revenue path | Add-on | Minimal | Selective |
| Professional/commercial | Supported | Pro/Team | Important | None or minimal | Important |
| Episodic intensive | Supported | Optional | Main revenue path | Optional | Selective |
| At-risk users | Protected | Deferred | Deferred | Reduced | Reactivation-oriented |
| New users | Protected | Delayed | Delayed | None initially | None initially |

This is intentionally asymmetric. The company should not expect advertising to monetize every free user, nor subscriptions to monetize every power user. The portfolio works because each mechanism is applied where its economics are strongest.

---

# 10. Governance and operating cadence

A monthly monetization council should include Product, Finance, Growth, Data Science, Customer Support, Trust and Safety, and Partnerships.

It should review:

- Revenue and contribution by segment;
- Cohort retention;
- Experiment results;
- Rating and review trends;
- Billing and support complaints;
- Competitor pricing and packaging;
- Infrastructure cost per high-usage user;
- Partner economics;
- Exceptions and rollback decisions.

Each experiment should have:

- A hypothesis;
- A defined target segment;
- A control group;
- A primary metric;
- Guardrail metrics;
- A maximum exposure;
- A stop condition;
- A decision date;
- An owner.

The organization should preserve the ability to roll back quickly. That may be more valuable than extracting the last increment of short-term revenue from a treatment that is beginning to damage trust.

---

# Recommended first 90 days

## Days 1–30: establish the fact base

- Complete behavioral segmentation;
- Calculate contribution by segment and usage intensity;
- Identify premium-feature demand and limit-reach events;
- Audit the prior paywall and ad tests;
- Instrument billing, churn, uninstall, and ratings data;
- Set experiment stop thresholds;
- Select a persistent control cohort.

## Days 31–60: launch low-friction monetization

- Introduce Plus and Pro packaging to selected cohorts;
- Test credit packs and short-term passes for episodic intensive users;
- Offer ad-free access as a meaningful Plus benefit;
- Remove or limit intrusive ad placements;
- Begin behavior-triggered upgrade prompts;
- Start one sponsored-access or distribution partnership pilot.

## Days 61–90: expand only validated approaches

- Compare segment-level contribution, not headline conversion;
- Extend successful subscription offers through a full billing cycle;
- Test opt-in rewarded access with strict frequency caps;
- Begin a small Team or Business pilot for commercial users;
- Refine limits and credit prices based on observed willingness to pay;
- Roll back any treatment associated with retention, rating, or trust deterioration.

---

# Final recommendation

The app should adopt a **segmented monetization portfolio**, not a third attempt at broad extraction from the entire free base.

The recommended sequence is:

1. **Protect the free core experience** and remove intrusive ad patterns.
2. **Introduce a simple Free / Plus / Pro ladder** based on convenience, advanced capability, and professional outcomes.
3. **Use credits, passes, and metered overage** for episodic or unpredictable high-intensity users.
4. **Apply carefully capped, contextual, or opt-in advertising** primarily to users who prefer not to subscribe.
5. **Use partnerships** for sponsored access, enterprise distribution, integrations, and relevant commerce.
6. **Trigger offers from demonstrated value**, particularly repeated limit reaches and advanced-feature usage.
7. **Evaluate experiments on retained contribution**, with app-store ratings, uninstall rates, task completion, and support complaints treated as hard guardrails.
8. **Maintain holdouts long enough to observe cancellation and reputation effects**, rather than optimizing on short-term conversion.

The most valuable shift may be conceptual: the company does not need to make every user pay. It needs to understand which users are receiving recurring value, which are receiving episodic value, and which are better monetized indirectly. A more patient and differentiated approach should produce a healthier revenue curve—and leave the product with something worth paying for.

# Monetization Strategy: Price the Value, Preserve the Habit

## Executive recommendation

The app should not attempt to solve its losses with a single harder paywall or a large advertising rollout. Both approaches would monetize all free users similarly even though usage data shows they receive very different value.

The recommended model is a **segmented, four-part monetization architecture**:

1. **Keep a useful, habit-forming free tier** with predictable limits and no surprise loss of existing core functionality.
2. **Offer two subscription tiers**:
   - A moderately priced **Plus** tier for engaged mainstream users;
   - A higher-priced **Pro** tier for power users whose usage, productivity, or outcomes justify a premium.
3. **Use passes or usage-based charges selectively** for bursty demand and costly premium actions—not for routine core use.
4. **Introduce restrained, opt-in or low-interruption advertising and partnerships** primarily for low-propensity free users, with strict frequency, privacy, and quality controls.

The app should optimize for:

\[
\textbf{Net revenue contribution per active user}
\]

subject to hard retention, uninstall, rating, refund, and customer-trust guardrails.

The first objective should not be to maximize immediate conversion. It should be to increase monetized participation across subscriptions, one-time purchases, advertising, and partnerships while preserving the free user base that generates habit, network effects, referrals, data, and future conversion.

Reasonable 12-month directional goals—subject to current pricing and category economics—are:

- Increase paid subscriber conversion from under 2% to approximately **3–4%**;
- Generate additional revenue from **5–15% of non-subscribing users** through passes, rewarded ads, or partnerships;
- Increase revenue per monthly active user by **30–60%**;
- Improve contribution margin per active user after payment, serving, support, ad, and partner costs;
- Keep treatment-induced D30 retention deterioration below approximately **1–2% relative**, with tighter limits for mature high-value cohorts;
- Avoid a statistically and practically meaningful increase in uninstall rate, one- and two-star reviews, refunds, or support complaints.

These should be portfolio targets. A power-user segment may support much higher conversion, while casual users may be better monetized lightly or not at all.

---

# 1. Establish the governing economics

## North-star metric

Use:

\[
\text{Net contribution per active user}
=
\text{subscription revenue}
+
\text{usage revenue}
+
\text{advertising revenue}
+
\text{partnership revenue}
-
\text{platform fees}
-
\text{variable serving cost}
-
\text{ad and partner costs}
-
\text{payment losses}
-
\text{incremental support cost}
\]

The denominator should include all active users, not just subscribers, to prevent a strategy that extracts more from a small group while shrinking the overall user base.

Track complementary metrics:

- Net contribution per retained user;
- Lifetime value by acquisition source and segment;
- Paid conversion and retained paid conversion;
- Revenue per payer;
- Revenue per nonpayer;
- Incremental revenue after cannibalization;
- Payback period;
- Subscriber churn and downgrade rate;
- Free-to-paid-to-free movement;
- Cost to serve by user segment.

## Evaluate lifetime impact, not launch-week revenue

For each monetization treatment, estimate:

\[
\text{Incremental lifetime value}
=
\text{incremental monetization}
-
\text{lost future value from churn}
-
\text{incremental costs}
-
\text{cannibalized revenue}
\]

The earlier paywall test likely optimized a short-term metric—such as trial starts or checkout views—while ignoring uninstall and retention losses. Advertising tests similarly captured immediate impressions while ratings reflected accumulated irritation.

No test should be declared successful based only on first-week revenue.

---

# 2. Build actionable user segments

The exact segment definitions should be learned from the app’s data, but the monetization system should distinguish at least the following behavioral groups. Users can transition between groups as their behavior changes.

## Segment A: Casual or exploratory users

**Signals**

- Low session frequency;
- Limited feature breadth;
- Short tenure or inconsistent activity;
- Low completion of the app’s primary value event;
- Weak response to previous offers.

**Value received**

These users are still learning the product or use it occasionally. They are unlikely to justify a recurring subscription.

**Recommended monetization**

- Useful free experience;
- No early hard paywall;
- Limited contextual sponsorship or low-frequency advertising after activation;
- Optional rewarded ads for a temporary premium action;
- Low-priced day, week, or project pass when a need arises;
- Education about premium value only after the user experiences a relevant benefit.

The primary objective is activation and habit formation, not immediate subscription conversion.

---

## Segment B: Habitual mainstream users

**Signals**

- Consistent weekly use;
- Repeated completion of the core value event;
- Use of several features;
- Increasing saved content, history, personalization, or workflow investment;
- Moderate but not extreme usage.

**Value received**

These users have established a habit and are the main audience for a standard paid plan.

**Recommended monetization**

- Plus subscription;
- Monthly and discounted annual options;
- Personalized paywall tied to features already used or repeatedly attempted;
- Free trial only when the user has sufficient activation and a clear premium use case;
- Ad-free experience as one benefit, but not the sole paid benefit;
- Family or household option if multiple-user value exists.

This segment should drive most of the increase in paid conversion.

---

## Segment C: Power or professional users

**Signals**

- High frequency and depth of use;
- Reliance on advanced workflows;
- High export, storage, creation, automation, collaboration, or processing activity;
- Measurable time, money, or performance benefits from the app;
- High cost to serve in some cases.

**Value received**

These users receive disproportionate utility and often have higher willingness to pay.

**Recommended monetization**

- Pro subscription priced materially above Plus;
- Advanced functionality, higher limits, priority processing, expanded storage, automation, integrations, or premium support;
- Annual plan with clear savings;
- Team or small-business upgrade where relevant;
- Metered overages only for expensive, scalable resources beyond a generous included allowance.

The company should not leave this group on a single low-priced consumer plan if the product creates professional or economic value for them.

---

## Segment D: Bursty or project-based users

**Signals**

- Long periods of low activity interrupted by intense use;
- Seasonal, travel, event, exam, project, or life-stage usage;
- Repeated subscription cancellation soon after joining;
- Strong use of one premium feature but little recurring engagement.

**Value received**

The user may have high willingness to pay at a particular moment but poor fit with a recurring subscription.

**Recommended monetization**

- Day, week, month, or project pass;
- Packs of premium actions or credits;
- A one-month non-renewing option where app-store rules permit;
- Clear upgrade credit if the user later buys a subscription;
- Subscription offer only after repeated bursts suggest recurring value.

This captures revenue that a subscription-only model loses while reducing immediate cancellation and refund frustration.

---

## Segment E: Value-seeking, ad-tolerant free users

**Signals**

- Long tenure and meaningful usage;
- Repeated refusal of subscription offers;
- Price sensitivity;
- Willingness to watch rewarded placements or engage with relevant offers;
- Low likelihood of converting under realistic subscription pricing.

**Value received**

The user values the app but does not assign enough value to a recurring payment.

**Recommended monetization**

- Limited native or contextual advertising;
- Rewarded advertising that unlocks a temporary feature or allowance;
- Sponsored content or benefits with clear labeling;
- Partner-funded access;
- Regional or student pricing where willingness to pay, rather than lack of value, is the constraint.

Do not repeatedly show subscription prompts to users who have consistently declined. Monetize them through a lighter channel.

---

## Segment F: At-risk or low-satisfaction users

**Signals**

- Falling engagement;
- Failed tasks;
- Repeated errors;
- Poor support interactions;
- Recent negative feedback;
- High likelihood of uninstalling.

**Recommended monetization**

- Suppress advertising and aggressive paywalls;
- Resolve product friction;
- Offer support, education, or a win-back experience;
- Monetize only after the user again receives consistent value.

Attempting to extract revenue from a frustrated user can turn ordinary churn into a negative review and reputational damage.

---

# 3. Design the product and pricing architecture

## A. Preserve a coherent free tier

The free tier must remain good enough to demonstrate the app’s core promise and sustain organic growth.

It should provide:

- The complete basic value loop;
- A useful allowance of routine activity;
- Access to saved work or user-created data;
- Predictable rather than arbitrary limits;
- No sudden retroactive removal of core features;
- A clear explanation of what is free and what is paid.

The free tier should not provide unlimited access to every high-value or high-cost capability. Appropriate boundaries include:

- Advanced rather than basic functionality;
- Higher capacity or speed;
- Automation and bulk operations;
- Premium customization;
- Cross-device, team, export, or integration features;
- Expensive processing or content;
- Professional controls;
- Convenience and priority.

The best boundary occurs after the user understands the value but before unlimited usage becomes economically destructive.

### Avoid these paywall patterns

- Paywalling before the first successful value event;
- Blocking access to user-created content without warning;
- Repeated full-screen interruptions;
- Artificial delays designed only to force payment;
- Misleading countdowns;
- Preselected annual plans without clear disclosure;
- Difficult cancellation;
- Trials that obscure renewal terms;
- Showing the same rejected offer every session.

These tactics may lift checkout starts while harming retention, refunds, ratings, and regulatory risk.

---

## B. Offer a simple two-tier subscription structure

### Free

- Full basic value loop;
- Reasonable routine allowance;
- Limited premium-feature previews;
- Restricted advanced capabilities;
- Light monetization only after activation.

### Plus

Designed for habitual mainstream users.

Potential benefits:

- Higher or unlimited routine limits, where economically viable;
- No third-party advertising;
- Full personalization or customization;
- Enhanced quality, history, synchronization, or offline access;
- Selected premium features;
- Faster or more convenient workflows;
- Standard customer support.

Plus should solve recognizable recurring problems rather than merely remove irritants artificially added to Free.

### Pro

Designed for power, professional, or high-cost users.

Potential benefits:

- Highest included allowances;
- Advanced workflows;
- Automation and bulk actions;
- Premium processing quality or speed;
- Integrations and exports;
- Collaboration or team features;
- Analytics;
- Priority support;
- Commercial-use rights where applicable.

Pro should be visibly differentiated from Plus. If the distinction is only a slightly higher limit, it may create confusion without increasing willingness to pay.

## C. Keep the menu small

The default purchase screen should generally show no more than:

- Plus monthly;
- Plus annual;
- Pro monthly or annual, when relevant to the user;
- A pass or credit alternative for identified bursty users.

Do not display every option to everyone. Progressive disclosure is preferable: show the offer most consistent with the user’s demonstrated behavior and provide a transparent comparison page for users who want alternatives.

---

# 4. Use usage-based pricing selectively

Pure usage-based pricing is usually unsuitable for a consumer app’s core experience. It creates anxiety, makes costs unpredictable, and can discourage the engagement the app needs.

Use it only when all of the following are true:

1. The unit of consumption is understandable;
2. The user perceives incremental value from each unit;
3. The activity has meaningful variable cost or premium value;
4. Users can see usage before incurring charges;
5. Spending caps and warnings are available;
6. The model is simpler or fairer than forcing a subscription.

Appropriate units could include:

- Premium generations or analyses;
- High-cost processing minutes;
- Large exports;
- Specialist reports;
- Storage beyond an included allowance;
- High-resolution or expedited operations;
- One-time event or project access.

## Recommended implementation

- Include a generous monthly allowance in Plus and a larger one in Pro;
- Sell optional top-up packs;
- Let unused purchased credits remain valid for a reasonable period;
- Warn users at 50%, 80%, and 100% of allowance;
- Require explicit approval before paid overages;
- Offer spending caps;
- Never allow an unexpectedly large bill;
- Show the subscription alternative when repeated credit purchases make it cheaper.

Credits should be denominated in understandable actions wherever possible. An opaque virtual currency can create distrust and complicate price comparison.

---

# 5. Introduce advertising without repeating the ratings failure

Advertising should be a secondary monetization stream for users unlikely to subscribe, not the dominant experience for every free user.

## Recommended formats

In preference order:

1. **Rewarded advertising** initiated by the user in exchange for a meaningful, clearly stated benefit;
2. **Contextual native placements** integrated into natural browsing or discovery surfaces;
3. **Sponsored benefits or content**, visibly labeled and held to product-quality standards;
4. **Low-frequency display placements** in noncritical moments.

Avoid or severely restrict:

- Interstitials during a core task;
- Ads immediately after launch;
- Audio or video that starts automatically;
- Ads that resemble system messages or app controls;
- Ads adjacent to sensitive personal information;
- Repetitive, low-quality, or misleading creative;
- Ad formats that cause accidental taps;
- Advertising to children or sensitive groups without appropriate controls.

## Frequency and eligibility rules

- Do not show ads before the user completes initial activation;
- Suppress ads during critical workflows;
- Set per-session and per-day caps;
- Reduce frequency for users showing declining engagement;
- Remove third-party ads for subscribers;
- Suppress a subscription upsell immediately after a rewarded ad;
- Exclude sensitive contexts and categories;
- Give users controls over personalization where required;
- Never sell identifiable user data as the monetization model.

Ad load should be optimized against **incremental contribution after churn**, not fill rate or impressions.

A useful decision rule is:

\[
\text{Net ad value}
=
\text{ad revenue}
-
\text{lost subscription conversion}
-
\text{lost retention and referral value}
-
\text{support and reputation costs}
\]

Ad exposure should stop increasing when incremental revenue is outweighed by these costs.

---

# 6. Use partnerships for monetization and distribution

Partnerships can monetize price-sensitive users without degrading the interface, but only when the partner adds value.

## A. Bundled distribution

Potential partners include:

- Mobile carriers;
- Device manufacturers;
- Banks and card issuers;
- Employers and employee-benefit platforms;
- Educational institutions;
- Membership organizations;
- Complementary consumer services.

The partner can fund or subsidize Plus access for an eligible population. This produces wholesale recurring revenue and lowers acquisition cost.

Terms should address:

- Activation and retained-use payments;
- Renewal ownership;
- Customer support responsibility;
- Data-sharing limits;
- Brand presentation;
- Promotion commitments;
- Conversion after the funded period;
- Incrementality versus users who would have paid directly.

Avoid deals that appear large but merely replace full-price subscribers with discounted partner-funded accounts.

## B. Contextual commerce and affiliate partnerships

If the app naturally precedes a relevant purchase or booking decision, it can earn:

- Referral fees;
- Marketplace commissions;
- Lead-generation revenue;
- Revenue share on complementary services.

Recommendations must be relevant and clearly disclosed. Ranking should not become pay-to-play in a way that degrades product quality or user trust.

## C. Sponsored access

A partner may fund:

- Premium access for students or other eligible users;
- A limited premium feature;
- Educational or public-interest content;
- Seasonal passes;
- Rewards for completing a useful action.

The sponsor should not obtain sensitive user data without explicit, informed consent.

## D. Team or small-business expansion

If power users employ the app for work, add:

- Central billing;
- Seat management;
- Shared assets or workflows;
- Administrative controls;
- Team analytics;
- Business support.

This can create a higher-value revenue stream without increasing pressure on ordinary consumers.

---

# 7. Map offers to user moments

Offers should be triggered by demonstrated value, not simply time elapsed.

## High-quality trigger moments

- After the user completes the core value event several times;
- When a user tries an advanced feature;
- When approaching a clearly communicated allowance;
- After the app produces a valuable result;
- When the user begins using multiple devices or collaboration;
- When recurring burst usage indicates a subscription may now be economical;
- After a positive satisfaction signal;
- At a natural planning or renewal moment.

## Offer examples

### Casual user

“You have completed your first project. The free plan includes two more this month. Need more for a short-term project? Unlock a seven-day pass.”

### Habitual user

“You used the app on 12 days this month and saved 18 items. Plus adds full history, synchronization, and advanced tools.”

### Power user

“You are using bulk processing and exports each week. Pro includes larger allowances, automation, and priority processing.”

### Price-sensitive user

“Continue with Free, or watch an optional sponsor message to unlock one premium action.”

Every paywall should include an obvious path to continue on Free where the free experience remains available.

---

# 8. Optimize pricing without relying on blanket discounts

## Establish willingness to pay

Use:

- Behavioral demand tests;
- Price-sensitivity research;
- Existing conversion by geography and platform;
- Cancellation and downgrade surveys;
- Competitor and substitute analysis;
- Value-based interviews with power users;
- Tests of plan packaging separately from price.

Stated willingness to pay is not sufficient. Validate it through controlled purchase experiments.

## Pricing principles

- Anchor price to recurring user value, not only competitor prices;
- Maintain a meaningful annual discount, often equivalent to roughly two months free, but test the amount;
- Offer regional pricing where purchasing power and competitive conditions warrant it;
- Consider verified student or hardship pricing rather than permanent broad discounts;
- Grandfather or transition existing paying users carefully;
- Avoid frequent discounts that teach users never to pay full price;
- Use win-back discounts selectively for former subscribers, not active full-price users;
- Display billing cadence and renewal terms clearly.

When testing price, analyze both conversion and:

\[
\text{Retained revenue}
=
\text{price}
\times
\text{conversion}
\times
\text{renewal probability}
\]

A higher launch-month revenue result can be inferior if renewal or satisfaction falls.

---

# 9. Experiment plan

## Phase 0: Instrumentation and baseline

Before launching new monetization, build an event-level baseline covering:

- Activation and core value completion;
- Feature usage;
- Paywall impressions and context;
- Offer eligibility;
- Trial start, conversion, renewal, cancellation, refund, and charge failure;
- Ad eligibility, impression, completion, dismissal, and complaint;
- Pass and credit purchases and consumption;
- Uninstall proxies and confirmed uninstall where available;
- App-store rating and review text;
- Support contacts;
- Serving cost;
- Segment assignment and transitions.

Maintain a persistent global holdout—typically a small percentage of eligible users—who receive the existing experience. This reveals cumulative monetization effects that short experiments miss.

## Phase 1: Validate packaging and user moments

Test product packaging before increasing pressure.

Experiments should include:

1. Plus benefit bundles;
2. Pro benefit bundles for power users;
3. Monthly versus annual presentation;
4. Passes for bursty users;
5. Feature-triggered versus generic paywalls;
6. Timing after first, third, or later value events;
7. Personalized benefit copy versus generic feature lists;
8. Trial versus no-trial offers;
9. Exchange of a premium action for a rewarded ad.

Run these tests within defined segments rather than across the entire population.

## Phase 2: Test price and limits

After finding a credible package, test:

- Plus and Pro price points;
- Annual discounts;
- Free-tier allowances;
- Included Pro usage;
- Credit-pack sizes and prices;
- Regional pricing;
- Family or team options.

Do not simultaneously change price, feature bundle, paywall design, and free limits. Otherwise management cannot determine what caused the result.

## Phase 3: Test restrained advertising

Begin with a small eligible population of low-subscription-propensity free users.

Suggested sequence:

1. Rewarded placements only;
2. One native contextual placement with a low daily cap;
3. Alternative caps and locations;
4. Partner-sponsored benefits;
5. Broader rollout only if long-term retention and rating guardrails remain healthy.

Subscribers and high-propensity prospects should remain in separate test cells to measure subscription cannibalization.

## Phase 4: Test partnerships

Use geographic or eligibility-based pilots with:

- Incremental holdouts;
- Activation targets;
- Retained-use requirements;
- Direct-subscription cannibalization analysis;
- Support-cost measurement;
- Cohort retention after the sponsored period ends.

Do not evaluate a partnership on distributed licenses alone. Measure activated and retained users, net revenue, and post-partnership conversion.

## Phase 5: Optimize the portfolio

Use a contextual decision system only after causal tests establish which treatments work.

The system may select among:

- No offer;
- Plus;
- Pro;
- Pass;
- Credit pack;
- Rewarded ad;
- Partner benefit;
- Support intervention.

It should optimize expected long-term contribution subject to exposure caps and user-level guardrails. Machine learning should improve assignment, not justify showing more monetization pressure.

---

# 10. Experiment design and decision rules

## Randomization

Randomize at the user or account level, not session level, so one user does not experience conflicting paywalls and prices. Where social or network effects are strong, use cluster or geographic randomization.

Stratify by:

- User segment;
- Tenure;
- Platform;
- Geography;
- Acquisition source;
- Existing engagement;
- Prior monetization exposure;
- Predicted conversion and churn risk.

## Duration

Experiments should cover at least:

- One full weekly usage cycle;
- Enough time for D7 and D30 retention where material;
- A renewal event for trial and subscription tests;
- Longer windows for annual plans, supported by leading indicators and later cohort validation.

Short tests may be used to stop harmful treatments, but not to approve full rollout.

## Rollout sequence

A safe progression is:

- Internal and employee testing;
- 1% of eligible users;
- 5%;
- 10–20%;
- 50%;
- Full rollout with persistent holdout.

Each stage should require both revenue improvement and guardrail clearance.

## Evaluate incremental value

For each treatment, report:

- Incremental paid conversion;
- Incremental revenue per eligible user;
- Incremental contribution per eligible user;
- Subscription cannibalization;
- Pass or credit cannibalization;
- D1, D7, D30, and later retention;
- Uninstalls;
- Rating and review impact;
- Refund and cancellation impact;
- Heterogeneous effects by segment.

Use intent-to-treat analysis as the primary result. Looking only at users who viewed or clicked an offer can overstate value because those users self-selected.

---

# 11. Guardrails and early-warning system

App-store ratings are a lagging indicator. The company needs session-, day-, and cohort-level signals that detect damage earlier.

## Immediate experience guardrails

Monitor within hours or days:

- Paywall close rate;
- Repeated paywall exposure;
- Blocked-action abandonment;
- Session termination immediately after a paywall or ad;
- Ad dismissal and accidental-click rate;
- Core-task completion;
- Error and latency rates;
- Support contacts per 1,000 exposed users;
- Negative in-app feedback;
- Notification opt-outs;
- Trial cancellation within 24 hours;
- Refund requests.

These reveal friction before formal churn appears.

## Retention guardrails

Track by treatment and segment:

- Next-day return;
- D7, D14, D30, and D90 retention;
- Active days per user;
- Core value events per retained user;
- Session frequency;
- Cohort survival;
- Free-user reactivation;
- Subscriber renewal and voluntary churn;
- Downgrade rate;
- Uninstall rate.

Retention should be evaluated both in absolute terms and relative to control.

## Reputation guardrails

Track:

- Daily app-store rating;
- One- and two-star review rate;
- Review sentiment mentioning ads, price, paywalls, cancellation, or lost features;
- Rating by app version and geography;
- Social-media complaint volume;
- Support escalation rate;
- Payment disputes and chargebacks.

Use a review-classification system to identify emerging monetization complaints within 24 hours.

## Trust and fairness guardrails

Monitor:

- Unexpected-charge complaints;
- Cancellation completion time;
- Refund rate;
- Price inconsistency complaints;
- Ads shown in excluded contexts;
- Sensitive-category ad violations;
- Privacy opt-outs and deletion requests;
- False targeting of at-risk or ineligible users;
- Accessibility failures;
- Child-safety or regulatory incidents.

## Suggested stop rules

Exact thresholds should reflect baseline variance, but examples include:

- Immediate pause for any material privacy, safety, billing, or regulatory issue;
- Pause if uninstall rate rises more than approximately **5% relative** during the early window;
- Pause if D7 retention falls more than approximately **2% relative**, unless a preapproved segment-specific threshold applies;
- Pause if D30 retention falls more than approximately **1–2% relative**;
- Pause if one- and two-star reviews rise materially for several consecutive days;
- Pause if refund or chargeback rates exceed policy or platform thresholds;
- Reduce ad frequency if core-task completion or sessions per retained user decline materially;
- Reject treatments whose revenue gain disappears after estimated churn and cannibalization costs.

Statistical significance should not be the only criterion. A small but persistent retention decline across millions of users can be economically serious even before it reaches conventional significance.

---

# 12. Governance and operating model

Create a monetization council with representatives from:

- Product;
- Growth;
- Finance;
- Data science;
- Customer support;
- Brand;
- Advertising operations;
- Privacy and legal;
- Engineering;
- App-store operations.

Its responsibilities should include:

- Approving entitlement and price changes;
- Setting exposure caps;
- Reviewing experiment results;
- Monitoring global holdouts;
- Investigating rating and complaint spikes;
- Ensuring cancellation and billing clarity;
- Preventing overlapping experiments;
- Reviewing effects on vulnerable or sensitive user groups.

Product and growth teams should not be rewarded on conversion alone. Their scorecards should include:

- Incremental long-term contribution;
- Retained paid conversion;
- Free-user retention;
- App-store ratings;
- Refunds and complaints;
- Monetization exposure;
- Customer lifetime value.

---

# 13. Twelve-month roadmap

## First 30 days: Establish control

- Stop broad increases in paywall or ad pressure;
- Define the behavioral segments;
- Build the contribution and retention baseline;
- Audit all monetization surfaces;
- Identify the core free value loop;
- Create global and segment-level holdouts;
- Implement daily ratings, review, uninstall, and support monitoring;
- Interview subscribers, former subscribers, power users, and long-tenured nonpayers.

## Days 30–90: Build the offer architecture

- Define Free, Plus, and Pro entitlements;
- Build passes or credits for bursty usage;
- Create feature-triggered paywalls;
- Add clear billing and cancellation flows;
- Test pricing research and prototype offers;
- Pilot rewarded advertising;
- Establish partner criteria and begin negotiations;
- Launch packaging tests in selected segments.

## Months 3–6: Run controlled monetization pilots

- Launch Plus to habitual users;
- Launch Pro to power users;
- Test annual and monthly plans;
- Pilot project passes and credit packs;
- Introduce low-frequency contextual ads to low-propensity users;
- Suppress monetization for at-risk cohorts;
- Launch one bundled-distribution partnership;
- Measure D30 effects before broad rollout.

## Months 6–12: Scale proven treatments

- Roll out successful subscriptions by segment;
- Expand rewarded and native ads only within validated caps;
- Add family, team, regional, or student plans where supported by evidence;
- Optimize included usage and top-up economics;
- Expand partner distribution;
- Add personalized offer selection with explicit pressure limits;
- Reprice or retire offers with poor retained economics;
- Continue using a global holdout to identify cumulative harm.

---

# Final recommendation

The app should replace uniform monetization pressure with a **segmented value exchange**:

- Casual users receive time to activate and can buy a temporary pass when needed.
- Habitual users receive a clear Plus subscription tied to recurring benefits.
- Power users receive a differentiated Pro tier aligned with the greater value they obtain.
- Bursty users can buy credits or short-term access instead of accepting an ill-fitting subscription.
- Long-tenured price-sensitive users see restrained, optional, or contextual advertising and partner-funded benefits.
- At-risk users receive product support rather than more monetization pressure.

Subscriptions should remain the largest and highest-quality revenue stream, but they should be supplemented by carefully bounded usage purchases, advertising, and partnerships. The free tier should continue delivering the app’s basic promise because it is the acquisition, engagement, referral, and future-conversion engine.

The central operating rule is:

> **Monetize after demonstrated value, in proportion to value received, and never approve a revenue gain until its retention, reputation, and cannibalization effects are known.**

That approach can materially raise revenue per user without repeating the aggressive paywall and intrusive-ad failures that damaged the app previously.

# Legacy Product End-of-Life and Migration Plan

The legacy product should not be treated as a declining asset to be switched off quickly. It remains economically important, operationally risky, and strategically useful as a source of customer relationships. The objective is to **move customers to the replacement product without allowing the legacy platform to become a permanent second product line**.

The program should follow this principle:

> Preserve customer outcomes, not every historical implementation detail.

A credible plan would use a **30–36 month sunset**, with earlier milestones for stopping new commitments and reducing technical exposure. The date should be announced only after the replacement product has passed defined capability and migration-readiness gates.

---

## 1. Program objectives

The end-of-life program should deliver five outcomes:

1. **Protect the installed revenue base**, particularly large and highly dependent customers.
2. **Move customers to the replacement product in controlled cohorts**, rather than through a single forced migration.
3. **Rebuild only capabilities that are economically and strategically necessary.**
4. **Reduce legacy security, maintenance, and talent risk on a predetermined schedule.**
5. **Create a firm end date**, with limited and expensive exceptions rather than an indefinite support commitment.

The business should measure the program on retained contribution, not simply the number of migrated accounts:

\[
\text{Migration contribution}
=
\text{retained customer gross profit}
-
\text{migration cost}
-
\text{incentives}
-
\text{temporary dual-run cost}
-
\text{incremental support cost}
\]

A migration that increases adoption of the replacement product but causes major enterprise churn may look successful in product reporting while damaging the company financially.

---

# 2. Recommended sunset timeline

A 36-month plan provides enough time to rebuild material gaps, migrate complex customers, and preserve negotiating leverage. The company should stop selling or expanding the legacy product considerably earlier than final shutdown.

## Phase 0: decision and preparation — Months 0–3

### Internal actions

- Name an executive owner and a dedicated migration office.
- Freeze new feature development on the legacy product.
- Permit only:
  - Security remediation;
  - Regulatory or contractual requirements;
  - Critical reliability fixes;
  - Migration-enabling interfaces.
- Inventory all customers, contracts, integrations, customizations, active modules, data volumes, usage patterns, and support dependencies.
- Create a complete capability comparison between legacy and replacement products.
- Identify customers with:
  - High revenue;
  - High switching risk;
  - Heavy use of niche features;
  - Complex integrations;
  - Contractual sunset restrictions;
  - Limited internal migration resources.
- Establish the baseline legacy cost, including engineering, infrastructure, support, security, and specialist-retention costs.

### Commercial actions

- Stop selling new standalone legacy contracts.
- Do not extend legacy contracts beyond the planned sunset date unless an exception is approved centrally.
- Stop promising feature parity that has not been tested.
- Begin confidential discussions with strategic accounts before public notice, particularly where renewal or procurement cycles are long.

At this stage, the company should quietly learn where the real resistance lies. The formal product catalog rarely reveals all of the operational dependencies.

---

## Phase 1: announce and prepare — Months 4–9

### Customer announcement

Issue a clear lifecycle notice that includes:

- The reason for the transition;
- The replacement product’s benefits;
- The planned end-of-sale date;
- The planned end-of-standard-support date;
- The final shutdown date;
- The migration assistance available;
- Data export and retention rights;
- The escalation path for customers with capability or timing concerns.

The notice should avoid language that implies the customer must immediately abandon a functioning system. A staged commitment will be more credible:

- “No new legacy functionality will be added.”
- “The replacement product is the strategic platform.”
- “We will support your migration through defined milestones.”
- “The legacy service will retire on [date], subject to the published readiness gates.”

### Product and engineering actions

- Deliver a migration-readiness assessment for every customer tier.
- Build a migration factory rather than handling each customer as a bespoke project.
- Establish compatibility test environments.
- Create APIs, export formats, transformation tools, and validation scripts.
- Begin rebuilding approved must-have capabilities.
- Publish a feature-gap register with status, owner, target date, and customer impact.

### Commercial actions

- Introduce migration terms at renewal.
- Offer customers a no-cost assessment and migration plan.
- Begin design-partner programs with a small group of representative customers.
- Include migration obligations and support boundaries in all new replacement-product contracts.

---

## Phase 2: early migration and capability validation — Months 10–18

- Migrate low-complexity and lower-risk customers first.
- Run controlled pilots with selected strategic customers.
- Complete the highest-priority feature rebuilds.
- Offer legacy and replacement products in parallel where operationally necessary.
- Begin charging for new legacy custom work and extended coexistence.
- Publish progress quarterly, including resolved capability gaps and successful migration references.
- Stop accepting new legacy integrations and major architecture changes.
- Require an executive-approved business case for any customer whose migration is not scheduled by Month 18.

By the end of this phase, the company should have evidence that the replacement product works in real customer environments, not only in demonstrations.

---

## Phase 3: migration at scale — Months 19–27

- Migrate the main body of standard customers.
- Move complex customers into named migration workstreams.
- Complete all approved parity or replacement solutions.
- Restrict legacy support to production stability, security, compliance, and migration assistance.
- Begin final-contract notifications for customers that have not committed to a migration date.
- Set final dates for data export, test environments, and dual-running.
- Increase commercial pricing for legacy-only use where contracts permit, reflecting its exceptional support and risk costs.

The company should not wait until this point to discover that several large accounts require twelve months of integration work. Those accounts should already have an agreed migration plan.

---

## Phase 4: final transition — Months 28–33

- Complete all standard migrations.
- Migrate strategic customers with executive sponsorship.
- Place remaining customers into one of three categories:
  1. Scheduled for shutdown;
  2. Approved for a short, paid exception;
  3. At material risk of defection and requiring an executive retention plan.
- Freeze all nonessential legacy access changes.
- Disable new customer provisioning and new modules.
- Conduct final disaster-recovery, security, data-export, and decommissioning tests.
- Confirm that replacement-product support capacity is adequate for the final migration wave.

---

## Phase 5: shutdown and controlled exceptions — Months 34–36

### Recommended dates

- **End of sale:** Month 3 or immediately after the program is announced.
- **End of new integrations and custom development:** Month 6.
- **End of standard support:** Month 30.
- **Final customer migration deadline:** Month 33.
- **Legacy service shutdown:** Month 36.

A short, paid exception period may be available after Month 36 only where all of the following apply:

- The customer has an executive-approved migration plan;
- A firm final shutdown date is contracted;
- The customer funds the incremental support and security cost;
- The exception does not require new feature development;
- The platform can be operated safely;
- The exception does not create material risk for other customers.

Exceptions should normally last no more than six months. They should become progressively more expensive and operationally narrower. A “temporary” exception that is priced like ordinary support will tend to become permanent.

---

# 3. Support policy

The support policy should become more restrictive in stages, but it must remain explicit and contractually consistent.

| Period | Support policy |
|---|---|
| Months 0–6 | Normal support; critical fixes and security patches; no new features |
| Months 7–18 | Normal production support; security and severity-one fixes; migration support prioritized |
| Months 19–30 | Limited support; critical incidents, security, compliance, and migration blockers only |
| Months 31–36 | Extended transition support; no routine enhancement work; pre-approved incident response |
| After Month 36 | No standard support; only contracted paid exceptions if technically safe |

## Severity policy

- **Severity 1:** Production outage, material data integrity issue, or critical security exposure — response and remediation.
- **Severity 2:** Significant production degradation with a documented workaround — response, workaround, and migration priority.
- **Severity 3:** Minor defect or usability issue — documentation or workaround only.
- **Feature request:** Declined on the legacy platform; considered only for the replacement product if strategically justified.

## Security policy

Security risk should not be allowed to determine the shutdown date by surprise. The company should:

- Continue critical security patches through the final support period;
- Conduct regular vulnerability scanning and penetration testing;
- Remove unsupported third-party dependencies;
- Restrict access and administrative privileges;
- Isolate legacy infrastructure where possible;
- Require compensating controls for customers with extended exceptions;
- Maintain an incident-response plan through final decommissioning;
- Destroy or archive data according to contract and legal requirements after shutdown.

If a critical vulnerability cannot be safely mitigated, the company should reserve the right to accelerate an affected component’s retirement, with advance notice where practicable and a documented customer-continuity plan.

---

# 4. Decide what to rebuild, replace, or retire

Not every niche legacy capability deserves direct parity. The company should evaluate each capability using both customer importance and strategic fit.

## Capability classification framework

For every legacy feature, collect:

- Number of active customers using it;
- Revenue and gross profit associated with those customers;
- Frequency and criticality of use;
- Whether it is required for regulatory, contractual, or safety reasons;
- Availability of a workaround;
- Migration complexity;
- Replacement-product architecture fit;
- Security and maintenance burden;
- Competitive differentiation;
- Likelihood of customer defection if removed.

A useful prioritization score is:

\[
\text{Capability priority}
=
\text{customer value}
\times
\text{revenue at risk}
\times
\text{migration criticality}
-
\text{build and maintenance cost}
\]

The result should be reviewed with customer-facing teams rather than decided solely by engineering or product management.

## Decision categories

### A. Rebuild in the replacement product

Rebuild when the capability:

- Is used by multiple strategic customers;
- Supports a high-value or mission-critical workflow;
- Creates meaningful switching risk;
- Is legally or contractually required;
- Fits the replacement product’s long-term architecture;
- Can be implemented without recreating the legacy platform’s complexity.

These items should receive named owners and committed release dates before the final migration waves.

### B. Replace with a modern workflow

Some legacy features should not be recreated literally. Instead, provide:

- A different user interface;
- An API or integration;
- A report or export;
- A workflow using several replacement-product functions;
- A partner-supported capability;
- A managed service operated outside the core product.

This is often the better answer where customers depend on the outcome but not the historical implementation.

### C. Retire with an explicit alternative

Retire a capability when:

- Usage is narrow and declining;
- It has no regulatory or contractual necessity;
- It is expensive or unsafe to maintain;
- A reasonable workaround exists;
- Customers can be given tools, services, or commercial support to adapt.

Retirement should be communicated as a documented product decision, not discovered during migration.

### D. Preserve temporarily through a bridge

For a small number of high-value features, use a bridge:

- Read-only legacy access;
- An export or conversion service;
- A separately hosted compatibility module;
- A partner integration;
- A limited API;
- A paid managed service with a fixed retirement date.

A bridge is acceptable when it buys migration time. It is not a substitute for deciding.

## Capability council

Create a temporary council including:

- Product;
- Engineering;
- Security;
- Customer success;
- Sales;
- Finance;
- Legal;
- Support;
- Representatives from strategic customers.

Each capability decision should have:

- Decision category;
- Affected customers;
- Revenue at risk;
- Replacement or workaround;
- Delivery owner;
- Target date;
- Migration dependency;
- Customer communication plan.

The council should be able to say “no” to parity requests that preserve old complexity without protecting meaningful customer value.

---

# 5. Segment customers by migration risk and value

Revenue tier alone is not enough. A small customer with a mission-critical niche dependency may be more likely to defect than a large customer using only standard workflows.

## Recommended customer segments

| Segment | Characteristics | Primary treatment |
|---|---|---|
| Strategic enterprise | High revenue, high switching cost, complex integrations, contractual influence | Executive sponsorship and named migration program |
| High-risk niche | Heavy dependence on missing legacy capabilities | Capability solution, design partnership, tailored retention plan |
| Standard commercial | Moderate revenue and repeatable workflows | Self-service tooling and structured migration package |
| Low-complexity / long tail | Limited data, integrations, or customization | Automated migration and group support |
| Contract-constrained | Renewal, procurement, or regulatory timing limits | Contract-specific transition plan |
| Defection watchlist | Competitive activity, complaints, stalled migration, or low replacement-product confidence | Executive intervention and save plan |

Each account should receive a migration score based on:

- Annual revenue and margin;
- Product dependency;
- Number and age of integrations;
- Data volume and complexity;
- Feature gaps;
- Customer health;
- Renewal date;
- Competitor activity;
- Internal customer capacity;
- Executive relationship strength.

The top-risk accounts should have named owners and weekly or biweekly review until their migration path is accepted.

---

# 6. Migration tooling and incentives by tier

## Strategic enterprise customers

### Tooling

Provide:

- A dedicated migration architect;
- Joint discovery and target-state design;
- Sandbox and test tenant;
- Automated data extraction and transformation;
- Reconciliation reports;
- API and integration assessment;
- Parallel-run capability;
- Cutover rehearsal;
- Executive readiness review;
- Hypercare for 60–90 days after cutover.

Where needed, establish a joint migration office with the customer.

### Incentives

Offer selectively:

- Migration services credits;
- Waived implementation or onboarding fees;
- Temporary price protection;
- Contract extension or co-terming;
- Replacement-product credits during dual running;
- Discounted first-year subscription;
- Priority roadmap consideration for broadly valuable missing capabilities;
- Service-level commitments;
- Executive governance and quarterly business reviews.

Avoid promising to rebuild a feature solely for one customer unless the customer funds a substantial portion of the work or the capability has broad strategic value.

## High-risk niche customers

### Tooling

- Feature-specific migration playbooks;
- Guided configuration;
- Conversion utilities;
- Compatibility reports;
- Partner or professional-services assistance;
- A documented workaround;
- Early access to rebuilt capabilities;
- A formal acceptance test.

### Incentives

- Free or discounted migration assessment;
- Limited-term access to both platforms;
- Credits for converting data or workflows;
- Contractual commitment to a replacement capability;
- Temporary support for the workaround;
- Renewal protection if the customer participates in a design-partner program.

The critical point is to make the customer’s operational risk visible and manageable. A feature checklist alone will not provide that confidence.

## Standard commercial customers

### Tooling

- Self-service migration wizard;
- Automated data mapping;
- Preflight validation;
- Data-quality report;
- Migration-status dashboard;
- Guided setup;
- Knowledge base and webinars;
- Scheduled cutover slots;
- Automated rollback where technically possible.

### Incentives

- Free migration;
- A limited replacement-product trial;
- Introductory pricing;
- Credit for unused legacy subscription time;
- Discount for early migration;
- Annual-plan discount only where the customer has completed a successful trial.

## Long-tail customers

### Tooling

- One-click or near-one-click migration;
- Default configuration templates;
- Bulk export and import;
- Automated email sequence;
- Group training sessions;
- Simple support escalation path;
- Final self-service migration deadline.

### Incentives

- Free migration;
- Short-term free access to the replacement product;
- No-cost data conversion;
- Small service credit rather than a complex negotiated discount.

## Contract-constrained customers

For customers whose contracts or regulatory cycles prevent normal migration:

- Map contractual obligations at the beginning of the program;
- Provide a written transition amendment;
- Offer a paid extension only when safe and time-bound;
- Align shutdown with renewal, audit, or certification dates where possible;
- Avoid allowing contract ambiguity to extend the platform indefinitely.

---

# 7. Retention plan for likely defectors

The customers most likely to leave should not receive only a larger discount. Their concern is usually a combination of capability risk, disruption, internal effort, and confidence in the vendor.

## A. Create a defection-risk model

Refresh the model monthly using:

- Legacy usage intensity;
- Missing-feature exposure;
- Customer support sentiment;
- Executive engagement;
- Renewal timing;
- Migration progress;
- Competitive procurement activity;
- Number of unresolved defects;
- Adoption of the replacement product;
- Data or integration complexity;
- Payment or contract friction.

Classify accounts into:

- **Green:** migration accepted and progressing;
- **Amber:** unresolved dependency or weak engagement;
- **Red:** active threat, stalled plan, or credible competitor alternative.

The red list should be short and owned at executive level.

## B. Give each red account a written save plan

Every plan should specify:

1. The customer’s business-critical outcomes;
2. The precise reason they may defect;
3. The capability or migration dependency involved;
4. The replacement solution;
5. The responsible internal owner;
6. The commercial offer;
7. The next customer milestone;
8. The final decision date;
9. The fallback if the preferred plan fails.

## C. Use a “migration assurance package”

For selected strategic and high-risk accounts, provide:

- Named executive sponsor;
- Named migration architect;
- Joint migration plan;
- Contractual service commitments;
- Demonstration using the customer’s own data or workflow;
- Parallel operation for an agreed period;
- Data reconciliation and sign-off;
- Dedicated hypercare;
- Escalation rights;
- Exit and data-export commitments.

This is likely to protect more value than indiscriminate discounting.

## D. Offer commercial protection without creating permanent dependency

Possible measures include:

- Price protection for the first renewal;
- Migration credits;
- Temporary dual-run credits;
- Waived implementation fees;
- Contract co-terming;
- A service credit for missed migration milestones;
- A fixed-cost migration package;
- Limited early termination rights if the committed replacement capability is not delivered.

Discounts should be linked to a migration milestone or commitment. For example, a customer may receive implementation credits after completing testing, rather than receiving a permanent price reduction simply for remaining on the legacy product.

## E. Use customer councils and references

Invite representative legacy customers to:

- Review feature priorities;
- Test migration tooling;
- Validate documentation;
- Participate in release previews;
- Provide reference stories after successful migration.

The council should not become a veto body. It is a way to identify practical risks before they appear during the final migration wave.

---

# 8. Migration factory and operating model

A repeatable migration factory should include four workstreams.

## 1. Product and capability

- Own feature-gap decisions;
- Deliver replacement capabilities;
- Publish compatibility and limitation documentation;
- Maintain the migration roadmap.

## 2. Data and tooling

- Build extraction, transformation, and loading tools;
- Validate completeness and integrity;
- Maintain rollback procedures;
- Support customer-specific mappings where justified.

## 3. Customer and commercial

- Segment accounts;
- Assign migration owners;
- Manage incentives and renewals;
- Track acceptance and cutover;
- Run retention interventions.

## 4. Platform and security

- Reduce legacy attack surface;
- Isolate infrastructure;
- Maintain supportability through shutdown;
- Execute final archival and decommissioning;
- Verify no production dependency remains.

Each migration wave should use a common sequence:

1. Discover;
2. Map dependencies;
3. Confirm capability coverage;
4. Run a test migration;
5. Reconcile data;
6. Conduct user acceptance testing;
7. Run parallel operations where required;
8. Cut over;
9. Provide hypercare;
10. Obtain customer sign-off;
11. Decommission legacy access for that customer.

---

# 9. Migration gates and decision rules

The company should not declare a customer “migrated” merely because a replacement account exists.

A customer should count as successfully migrated only when:

- Required data has been transferred and reconciled;
- Critical workflows have passed acceptance testing;
- Integrations are operational;
- Users have completed agreed training;
- The customer has run the replacement product through a normal business cycle;
- No severity-one or unresolved critical severity-two issue remains;
- The customer has approved cutover;
- Legacy access has been reduced or scheduled for termination.

## Platform-level shutdown gates

Before final shutdown, require evidence that:

- At least 95% of active revenue has migrated or has an approved exception;
- All red accounts have an executive disposition;
- No unresolved must-have capability remains without a documented alternative;
- Replacement-product availability and support performance meet agreed standards;
- Export and recovery procedures have been tested;
- Security exposure is within approved tolerance;
- Remaining legacy revenue is not being used to conceal unprofitable support;
- The financial benefit of shutdown is quantified and validated.

The final 5% may contain most of the complexity. Therefore, the 95% threshold should trigger final readiness review, not automatic shutdown.

---

# 10. Metrics and reporting

## Customer and commercial metrics

- Revenue retained;
- Gross profit retained;
- Churn and competitor displacement;
- Renewal rate by migration status;
- Net revenue retention;
- Migration acceptance rate;
- Migration completion rate;
- Time to migrate;
- Replacement-product expansion;
- Incentive cost per retained customer;
- Dual-run cost;
- Revenue at risk in red accounts.

## Product and operational metrics

- Capability coverage;
- Migration failure rate;
- Data reconciliation errors;
- Integration defects;
- Support volume;
- Severity-one and severity-two incidents;
- Replacement-product performance;
- Training completion;
- Customer satisfaction after cutover;
- Legacy infrastructure and support cost;
- Open security vulnerabilities;
- Specialist dependency and bus-factor risk.

## Executive dashboard

The dashboard should show, by segment and customer tier:

| Measure | Current | Target | Trend | Owner |
|---|---:|---:|---|---|
| Legacy revenue | — | — | — | Finance |
| Revenue migrated | — | — | — | Commercial |
| Revenue at risk | — | — | — | Customer Success |
| Red accounts | — | — | — | Executive Sponsor |
| Must-have gaps open | — | — | — | Product |
| Migration success rate | — | — | — | Migration Office |
| Legacy support cost | — | — | — | Engineering |
| Security exposure | — | — | — | Security |
| Replacement-product reliability | — | — | — | Platform |

A separate report should distinguish **customer migration completed** from **legacy infrastructure actually retired**. The latter often lags because of hidden dependencies.

---

# 11. Talent and knowledge-retention plan

The approaching retirement of legacy engineers is a program risk, not only a staffing issue.

Within the first 90 days:

- Identify all legacy subject-matter experts;
- Record architecture, deployment, recovery, and troubleshooting knowledge;
- Pair each specialist with at least one replacement-product engineer;
- Convert undocumented procedures into runbooks;
- Capture test cases and known customer-specific behaviors;
- Retain critical specialists through the final support period with targeted incentives;
- Use part-time advisory arrangements after retirement where appropriate;
- Prohibit any single expert from remaining the only person able to perform a critical operation.

Knowledge transfer should be measured through successful shadowing and recovery exercises, not document volume alone.

---

# 12. Financial treatment and investment discipline

The legacy product’s one-third contribution makes an overly generous migration program dangerous. However, underinvesting in migration could destroy that contribution through churn.

Create a dedicated migration P&L covering:

- Feature rebuild;
- Migration tooling;
- Customer success and professional services;
- Incentives;
- Dual-running infrastructure;
- Specialist retention;
- Security remediation;
- Replacement-product capacity;
- Lost legacy maintenance revenue;
- Retained and expanded replacement revenue;
- Avoided future legacy cost.

Evaluate each major investment using:

\[
\text{Migration ROI}
=
\frac{\text{retained contribution}+\text{avoided legacy cost}-\text{program cost}}
{\text{program cost}}
\]

The company should also calculate the cost of delay:

\[
\text{Cost of delay}
=
\text{additional legacy maintenance}
+
\text{security remediation}
+
\text{talent risk}
+
\text{continued dual-platform complexity}
\]

This makes the sunset decision more balanced. The choice is not between “migration cost” and “no cost”; delaying creates its own liability.

---

# 13. Governance and escalation

Establish a weekly program review during the first year and at least monthly thereafter.

Participants should include:

- Product;
- Engineering;
- Security;
- Finance;
- Sales;
- Customer Success;
- Support;
- Legal;
- Professional Services;
- Communications.

The migration office should have authority to:

- Prioritize migration capacity;
- Escalate unresolved customer blockers;
- Approve standard incentives within limits;
- Stop a migration wave when quality deteriorates;
- Recommend changes to the shutdown date based on defined gates.

The shutdown date should not move because a customer has not engaged. It should move only when there is evidence of a systemic readiness failure, such as a critical capability gap, unsafe security condition, inadequate replacement capacity, or material risk to a strategically important customer cohort.

---

# Immediate 90-day action plan

## Days 1–30

- Appoint the program owner;
- Freeze legacy feature development;
- Build the customer and capability inventory;
- Identify the top 25–50 accounts by revenue and defection risk;
- Establish the migration P&L;
- Start knowledge capture from legacy specialists;
- Define the proposed Month 36 shutdown date;
- Begin private strategic-account outreach.

## Days 31–60

- Complete feature classification;
- Approve rebuild, replace, bridge, or retire decisions for the highest-risk capabilities;
- Publish draft support and lifecycle policy;
- Select design-partner customers;
- Begin migration-tool development;
- Create account-level risk scores and save plans;
- Agree on standard incentives and exception rules.

## Days 61–90

- Announce the lifecycle plan;
- Launch pilot migrations;
- Release the first customer-facing migration assessment;
- Contractually align new renewals with the sunset;
- Begin rebuilding the highest-priority capabilities;
- Establish the executive dashboard;
- Test data export, transformation, and reconciliation;
- Confirm the first two migration waves.

---

# Final recommendation

Adopt a **36-month controlled retirement** with:

1. Immediate end of new legacy sales and feature development;
2. Standard support through Month 18;
3. Migration-focused and security-focused support through Month 30;
4. Final migration by Month 33;
5. Shutdown in Month 36;
6. Paid, time-limited exceptions only for customers with approved plans;
7. Rebuild of capabilities that protect material revenue, contractual commitments, or strategic workflows;
8. Replacement workflows, partners, or bridges for narrower needs;
9. Explicit retirement of low-value capabilities rather than indefinite parity work;
10. Tiered tooling, incentives, and executive retention plans;
11. A migration factory with measurable acceptance criteria;
12. A firm platform-level shutdown gate tied to customer outcomes, security, and economics.

The replacement product does not need to reproduce the legacy product feature for feature. It does need to make the customer’s important work reliable, explainable, and commercially safe to move. That distinction should quietly determine where investment goes—and where the company finally permits the old platform to end.

# Legacy Product End-of-Life and Migration Program

## Executive recommendation

The company should execute a **controlled, approximately 36-month sunset from formal announcement**, preceded by four to six months of preparation. It should not keep the legacy platform alive indefinitely because current profit obscures increasing security, staffing, and operational risk. It also should not force migration before the replacement product can support the few workflows that genuinely determine customer retention.

The recommended policy is:

- Stop new sales and custom development promptly.
- Maintain contractual support, critical security patches, and regulatory fixes during a defined standard-support period.
- Rebuild only niche capabilities that are economically material, broadly reusable, strategically aligned, and supportable on the replacement architecture.
- Address other gaps through workflow redesign, APIs, partners, export/archive tools, or explicit retirement.
- Give every customer a migration plan, readiness score, commercial offer, and named owner.
- Provide a limited, expensive extended-support period only for approved customers with funded migration plans.
- Establish a non-negotiable final shutdown date, subject only to narrowly defined legal or safety exceptions.

An illustrative calendar, assuming preparation begins in August 2026, is:

| Milestone | Target |
|---|---|
| Internal preparation and capability assessment | Aug. 2026–Jan. 2027 |
| Formal customer announcement; stop new sales | Feb. 2027 |
| Last date for new legacy deployments or material expansions | Aug. 2027 |
| Replacement closes all “must rebuild” gaps | By Jun. 2028 |
| Recommended migration deadline | Dec. 2028 |
| Standard support ends | Jan. 31, 2029 |
| Paid extended support ends | Jan. 31, 2030 |
| Platform shutdown and final data disposition | Jan. 31, 2030 |

These dates should be validated against contractual notice periods, product deployment models, regulatory retention obligations, customer budgeting cycles, and the engineering effort required for essential feature gaps. The final date should be changed only through executive and board-level exception governance—not account-by-account sales concessions.

The program’s objective is not merely to retire software. It is to:

1. Retain economically attractive customers;
2. Transfer recurring revenue and profit to the replacement platform;
3. Reduce security and operational exposure;
4. Preserve customer trust and data access;
5. Capture retiring engineers’ knowledge;
6. Prevent indefinite dual-platform costs.

---

# 1. Establish the decision baseline

Before announcing the sunset, build a customer-, capability-, and risk-level fact base.

## Customer economics

For every legacy customer, determine:

- Legacy recurring revenue and gross profit;
- Support, infrastructure, engineering, and account-management costs;
- Contract term, renewal date, notice requirements, and termination rights;
- Replacement-product revenue and margin potential;
- Expansion potential;
- Migration cost;
- Churn probability with and without migration support;
- Importance as a reference, ecosystem participant, or strategic account;
- Product usage by module and niche feature;
- Data volume, integrations, customizations, and compliance needs;
- Availability of viable competitors;
- Customer budget cycle and procurement lead time.

Legacy profitability should include hidden costs, not just current gross margin:

\[
\text{Risk-adjusted legacy contribution}
=
\text{legacy revenue}
-
\text{direct operating cost}
-
\text{support and engineering cost}
-
\text{expected security and outage loss}
-
\text{knowledge-concentration cost}
\]

Expected risk costs do not need false precision. Scenario estimates are enough to reveal whether reported profit is being sustained by underinvestment or unrecognized exposure.

## Technical and operational risk

Create a dependency inventory covering:

- Unsupported programming languages, libraries, databases, and operating systems;
- Third-party components approaching their own end of life;
- Known vulnerabilities and patch feasibility;
- Single points of failure;
- Disaster-recovery capability;
- Data-retention and deletion functions;
- Authentication, encryption, audit, and access-control limitations;
- Deployment dependencies;
- Customer-specific code forks;
- Manual operational procedures;
- Engineers with unique knowledge;
- Expected retirement and attrition dates.

Classify each risk as critical, high, medium, or low, with a named owner and latest safe operating date. The sunset date cannot extend beyond the point at which the company can no longer support the product safely and competently.

## Customer workflow telemetry

Do not treat customer statements about “required features” as sufficient evidence. Combine interviews with actual usage data:

- Number of customers using each niche feature;
- Frequency and depth of use;
- Users and business processes affected;
- Revenue and profit attached to the feature;
- Whether usage is increasing or declining;
- Whether a workaround exists;
- Whether the feature is required by law, contract, or audit;
- Whether competitors support it;
- Whether the need can be met through configuration, API, partner application, or process change.

Some legacy features appear indispensable because they have always existed, while others support a critical month-end, safety, compliance, or revenue-generating workflow used only occasionally. Frequency alone must not determine importance.

---

# 2. Set firm lifecycle stages and support policies

A clear lifecycle prevents customers and sales teams from interpreting “end of life” differently.

## Stage 1: Sustaining mode and announcement preparation

**August 2026–January 2027**

Immediately:

- Freeze discretionary legacy feature development;
- Permit only critical security, regulatory, reliability, and migration-enabling work;
- Stop creating new customer-specific forks;
- Capture knowledge from retiring engineers;
- Build automated data-export and migration tools;
- Complete customer segmentation and contract review;
- Identify capabilities that must be available before migration;
- Train sales, support, partners, and customer success on one message;
- Establish the exception process before customer negotiations begin.

Do not announce until the company can answer basic customer questions about dates, support, data, commercial terms, and missing capabilities.

## Stage 2: End of sale and active migration

**February 2027–December 2028**

At formal announcement:

- Stop sales to new customers;
- Stop new custom modules and major enhancements;
- Allow only limited additions for existing customers through August 2027 where contractually necessary;
- Continue standard support;
- Publish the migration calendar and capability roadmap;
- Assign migration waves;
- Offer migration assessments and incentives;
- Release tools, documentation, sandboxes, and validation reports.

Customers should be encouraged to complete migration by December 2028, leaving a buffer before standard support ends.

## Stage 3: End of standard support

**January 31, 2029**

After this date, legacy customers not in an approved extended-support program receive no:

- Routine defect corrections;
- Compatibility work for new browsers, operating systems, databases, or third-party applications;
- New integrations;
- Performance improvements;
- General advisory support;
- Service-level commitments beyond data extraction and shutdown assistance.

Any cloud-hosted operation during extended support should be isolated and tightly controlled.

## Stage 4: Paid extended support

**February 2029–January 2030**

Extended support is a transition mechanism, not an alternative product strategy. Eligibility should require:

- A signed migration plan with milestones;
- An executive sponsor on both sides;
- A target completion date before final shutdown;
- A supported technical configuration;
- No new custom functionality;
- Acceptance of reduced service scope;
- Payment reflecting the real cost and risk of continued support;
- Cooperation with security controls and migration testing.

Extended support should generally include:

- Critical exploitable security patches where technically feasible;
- Severity-one production incident response;
- Legally mandatory fixes where feasible;
- Data export and migration assistance.

It should exclude:

- Noncritical bugs;
- Certification on new technology stacks;
- Enhancements;
- New integrations;
- Cosmetic issues;
- Customer-specific customizations;
- Guaranteed remediation where the underlying technology cannot safely be patched.

Fees should increase materially—often by 25% to 50% or more—based on support burden and risk. They should fund the dedicated sustaining team and create an economic incentive to finish migration. Discounts should be exchanged only for accelerated, binding migration milestones.

## Stage 5: Final shutdown

**January 31, 2030**

At final end of life:

- Disable production access;
- Terminate hosting and routine support;
- Revoke credentials and integration endpoints;
- Deliver final agreed data exports;
- Preserve records required by law or contract;
- Delete remaining data under documented procedures;
- Provide deletion or disposition certificates where appropriate;
- Archive source code, build instructions, incident records, and customer configurations internally;
- Close residual infrastructure and vendor contracts.

For customers that need historical access but not active processing, offer a read-only archive, standardized export, or compliant data vault. This is preferable to operating the full legacy application indefinitely.

---

# 3. Decide which niche capabilities to rebuild

The company should not recreate the entire legacy product inside the replacement. That would import obsolete complexity and delay the strategic platform. Instead, place each feature into one of five dispositions:

1. **Rebuild natively;**
2. **Meet the outcome through configuration or workflow redesign;**
3. **Expose via API or partner solution;**
4. **Provide temporary migration compatibility;**
5. **Retire.**

## Capability decision scorecard

Score each niche capability against:

| Criterion | Questions |
|---|---|
| Revenue at risk | How much recurring revenue and profit depend on it? |
| Customer breadth | How many customers and users rely on it? |
| Workflow criticality | Does its absence stop a critical business process? |
| Compliance necessity | Is it required by law, regulation, safety, or audit? |
| Churn sensitivity | Would affected customers genuinely switch vendors? |
| Strategic fit | Does it belong in the replacement product’s long-term proposition? |
| Competitive relevance | Is it expected in credible alternatives? |
| Reusability | Can one implementation serve multiple customers? |
| Build and maintenance cost | What is the full lifecycle cost, not just initial development? |
| Architectural fit | Can it be delivered without compromising security or platform design? |
| Workaround quality | Can the outcome be achieved acceptably another way? |
| Time criticality | Must it exist before a given migration wave? |

A useful economic test is:

\[
\text{Expected retention value}
=
\text{affected contribution}
\times
\text{incremental retention probability}
\times
\text{expected retained life}
\]

Rebuild when expected retention and strategic value materially exceed development, support, and opportunity costs.

## Mandatory native rebuild

A capability should normally be rebuilt when it:

- Supports a legal, regulatory, safety, or audit requirement;
- Is essential to a critical workflow for multiple profitable customers;
- Has no practical workaround;
- Is strategically consistent with the replacement platform;
- Protects substantial renewable revenue;
- Can be delivered securely and maintained economically.

These features should receive committed dates, named product owners, customer design partners, and formal acceptance criteria.

## Workflow redesign or configuration

Do not reproduce a legacy interaction if the replacement achieves the same business outcome more safely or simply. Provide:

- Legacy-to-new workflow mapping;
- Role and permissions templates;
- Configuration packages;
- Training;
- Comparative reports;
- Customer sign-off on the new process.

The test is outcome equivalence, not pixel-level or step-level replication.

## API, partner, or extension solution

Use an extension approach when the requirement is legitimate but specialized:

- Industry-specific reports;
- Rare export formats;
- Specialized peripherals;
- Local compliance connectors;
- Unusual approval workflows;
- Niche analytics.

The company must still define support ownership, security requirements, certification, pricing, and contingency plans if a partner exits.

## Temporary compatibility bridge

For customers whose migration would otherwise be delayed, offer time-limited bridges such as:

- Translation adapters;
- Read-only legacy access;
- Parallel-reporting utilities;
- API facades;
- Scheduled data replication;
- Emulated output formats.

Every bridge should have an expiration date. Temporary middleware can otherwise become a second legacy platform.

## Retire

Retire features that are:

- Used by very few customers;
- Duplicative of safer or better workflows;
- Architecturally incompatible;
- Expensive to maintain relative to retained value;
- Unsupported by regulation or market demand;
- Available through reasonable export or partner alternatives;
- Primarily artifacts of old technical constraints.

Communicate retirement as an explicit product decision, explain the alternative, and give sufficient time for process change. Avoid leaving customers to discover missing functionality during final testing.

## Prevent single-customer vetoes

A large customer should not automatically force a native rebuild. For a highly specialized request, offer choices:

- Use the standard replacement workflow;
- Fund a reusable extension;
- Use an approved partner;
- Accept a temporary compatibility bridge;
- Export data and operate the function elsewhere.

Custom work should be accepted only if its price covers development and ongoing support, it does not compromise architecture, and the company is willing to maintain it beyond the immediate negotiation.

---

# 4. Segment customers and migration offers

Use customer value and migration complexity together rather than revenue alone.

| Tier | Typical profile | Service model |
|---|---|---|
| Tier 1: Strategic/high risk | Large profit pool, complex deployment, credible churn threat | Executive-sponsored, white-glove program |
| Tier 2: High value/standardizable | Material revenue, moderate complexity | Dedicated migration manager and structured services |
| Tier 3: Scaled commercial | Lower individual value, common configurations | Automated and partner-assisted migration |
| Tier 4: Low-value or uneconomic | Low revenue, high support burden, obsolete use | Self-service migration, paid assistance, or managed exit |
| Special regulated tier | Compliance, public-sector, safety, or data constraints | Controlled plan based on legal and assurance requirements |

## Tier 1: Strategic and high-risk customers

Provide:

- Named executive sponsor;
- Dedicated migration architect and program manager;
- Joint discovery and workflow mapping;
- Customer-specific gap analysis;
- Design-partner access for approved roadmap features;
- Sandbox and masked data rehearsal;
- Multiple migration rehearsals;
- Performance, security, and control validation;
- Integration remediation;
- Parallel run for an agreed period;
- Cutover command center;
- Enhanced post-go-live support for 60–90 days;
- Quarterly executive steering meetings.

Commercial incentives may include:

- Legacy-to-replacement price protection for a defined term;
- Migration service credits;
- Temporary dual-running rights without double subscription charges;
- Milestone-based implementation credits;
- Multi-year renewal incentives;
- Outcome-based service fees;
- Limited termination protection if committed must-have functionality is not delivered.

Incentives should require reciprocal commitments: signed migration dates, access to technical teams, timely testing, executive participation, and a replacement-platform contract.

## Tier 2: High-value, repeatable migrations

Provide:

- A named migration manager;
- Standard discovery workshops;
- Data and configuration assessment;
- Integration templates;
- Two rehearsal migrations;
- Training and adoption support;
- Standard cutover and rollback plans;
- 30–60 days of enhanced support.

Offer packaged service credits, temporary dual use, and modest term discounts tied to migration completion.

## Tier 3: Scaled commercial customers

Provide:

- Automated readiness assessment;
- Self-service migration portal;
- Standard data conversion;
- Guided checklists and videos;
- Group office hours;
- Certified implementation partners;
- Fixed-price migration packages;
- In-product prompts and progress tracking.

Incentives should be simple and deadline-based, such as free standard migration before a certain date, limited dual access, or a first-year replacement-platform credit. Avoid costly bespoke concessions.

## Tier 4: Low-value or uneconomic customers

Offer:

- Standard export tools;
- Documentation;
- Community or pooled support;
- Paid partner migration;
- A standard replacement plan;
- Clear shutdown deadlines.

If the replacement cannot serve them economically, provide a professional managed exit:

- Portable data exports;
- Reasonable transition cooperation;
- Referral to partners where appropriate;
- No punitive data-access restrictions;
- Clear deletion and archive procedures.

The goal is not to retain revenue that remains structurally loss-making after migration.

## Regulated and public-sector customers

Build plans around:

- Procurement and budget calendars;
- Validation and certification;
- Data residency;
- Records retention;
- accessibility requirements;
- Audit evidence;
- Security authorization;
- Change-control windows;
- Contractual notice obligations.

These customers may need earlier engagement and longer validation, not a later indefinite deadline.

---

# 5. Build migration tooling as a product

Manual migration will not scale and will increase error, cost, and customer anxiety. A dedicated migration engineering team should build repeatable tooling.

## Core tooling

The migration factory should include:

- Automated inventory of legacy data, modules, users, roles, integrations, and customizations;
- Readiness scoring;
- Legacy-to-new schema mapping;
- Data cleansing and deduplication;
- Automated extract, transform, and load pipelines;
- Data-volume and quality checks;
- Role and permission mapping;
- Configuration conversion;
- API and integration templates;
- Customization detection;
- Unsupported-feature reports;
- Reconciliation dashboards;
- Test-case generation;
- Dry-run environments;
- Cutover orchestration;
- Rollback or recovery procedures;
- Post-cutover monitoring;
- Audit logs and sign-off reports.

## Data reconciliation

For each migration, document:

- Record counts before and after;
- Financial or operational control totals;
- Missing, rejected, or transformed records;
- Permission equivalence;
- Historical-data accessibility;
- Integration message success;
- Report-output comparison;
- Customer acceptance.

Where the products use different data models, “100% identical” may not be meaningful. Define accepted transformation and reconciliation rules in advance.

## Migration playbooks

Create playbooks by configuration archetype rather than starting from zero for each customer:

- Standard cloud deployment;
- Highly customized enterprise deployment;
- On-premise installation;
- Regulated environment;
- Large historical-data estate;
- Heavy integration customer;
- Archive-only customer.

Each playbook should define prerequisites, responsibilities, duration, testing, rollback, communications, and acceptance.

## Security safeguards

Migration creates its own security risks. Require:

- Encryption in transit and at rest;
- Least-privilege temporary access;
- Time-limited credentials;
- Segregated migration environments;
- Approved handling of production data;
- Chain-of-custody records;
- Secure deletion of temporary copies;
- Independent testing for critical migration tools.

---

# 6. Retain customers most likely to defect

Create a “save desk” for accounts with both high value and high defection risk. It should include sales, customer success, product, migration engineering, commercial finance, legal, and an executive sponsor.

## Defection-risk model

Score customers using:

- Dependence on missing features;
- Number and depth of customizations;
- Migration complexity;
- Recent satisfaction and support history;
- Competitive procurement activity;
- Contract renewal timing;
- Executive relationship strength;
- Replacement-product adoption or trial behavior;
- Price sensitivity;
- Strategic importance;
- Competitor feature advantage;
- Change fatigue and internal customer resources.

Classify accounts as red, amber, or green, with weekly review for red accounts.

## Account-specific retention plan

Every red account should have:

1. A map of critical workflows and feature gaps;
2. A credible migration timeline;
3. Named product commitments, without vague roadmap promises;
4. A total-cost comparison against staying or switching;
5. An executive relationship plan;
6. Technical proof through a sandbox or pilot;
7. Commercial give-gets;
8. A competitor response strategy;
9. Escalation and recovery triggers;
10. A contingency plan if retention is not economic.

## Retention proposition

The message should focus on business outcomes, not the company’s desire to retire old technology. Demonstrate:

- Better security and compliance;
- Higher reliability and performance;
- Lower administration burden;
- New capabilities and future roadmap;
- Integration and automation improvements;
- Reduced infrastructure or upgrade costs;
- A migration plan that protects continuity;
- Long-term supportability.

For niche-feature-dependent customers, show a working prototype, partner solution, or validated redesigned workflow before asking for an irrevocable commitment.

## Commercial boundaries

Use incentives to offset legitimate migration friction, not to conceal product gaps. Possible tools include:

- Free or discounted migration services;
- Temporary subscription overlap;
- Price caps for one to three years;
- Training credits;
- Integration assistance;
- Milestone-based rebates;
- Adoption credits after successful go-live;
- Contract flexibility tied to delivery of defined must-have capabilities.

Avoid:

- Permanent discounts that destroy replacement economics;
- Open-ended free legacy support;
- Roadmap promises without engineering approval;
- Bespoke builds that recreate the legacy product;
- Paying customers to delay migration;
- Concessions without signed dates and customer obligations.

## Competitive defense

For accounts considering another vendor:

- Commission a rapid, evidence-based comparison;
- Quantify the cost, time, and risk of migrating to either platform;
- Address competitor advantages honestly;
- Demonstrate replacement-product references;
- Offer proof-of-value environments;
- Bring product and engineering leaders into critical discussions;
- Accelerate economically justified capabilities;
- Strengthen contractual commitments for approved deliverables.

Do not assume switching vendors is too difficult. The customer is already facing migration, which lowers the normal barrier to competitive replacement.

---

# 7. Manage profit transition explicitly

Because the legacy product generates one-third of current profit, the company needs a financial bridge by quarter.

Model:

- Legacy renewals and planned attrition;
- Extended-support revenue;
- Replacement subscription revenue;
- Migration-services revenue and cost;
- Incentives and discounts;
- Dual-running infrastructure;
- Feature-development investment;
- Retention and expansion;
- Support and hosting costs avoided;
- Decommissioning costs;
- Security-risk reduction.

A useful measure is:

\[
\text{Migrated customer value}
=
\text{replacement ARR}
\times
\text{replacement contribution margin}
-
\text{migration and incentive cost}
\]

Track both current-year profit and lifetime value. A migration may temporarily depress profit but improve lifetime economics.

## Protect the replacement business model

The company should not automatically carry every legacy price and entitlement forward. Segment commercial treatment:

- Protect pricing temporarily for strategic customers;
- Move customers to standard replacement packaging over time;
- Charge for incremental replacement capabilities where appropriate;
- Avoid creating permanent “legacy contracts on a new platform”;
- Put expiration dates on special terms.

## Resource the program separately

Create three protected capacity pools:

1. **Legacy sustaining team:** security, severe defects, and reliability only;
2. **Replacement gap team:** approved must-have capabilities;
3. **Migration platform team:** tooling, data conversion, and automation.

Without protected teams, urgent legacy incidents will repeatedly consume replacement development, while customer migrations will remain manual.

---

# 8. Address retiring-engineer and knowledge risk immediately

The engineer-retirement issue may create an earlier deadline than the commercial plan.

Actions should include:

- Retention agreements through defined knowledge-transfer milestones;
- Succession pairing between legacy experts and designated engineers;
- Recorded architecture and debugging sessions;
- Documentation of builds, deployments, integrations, data models, and failure modes;
- Incident simulations led by successor staff;
- Automated build and test environments;
- Recovery drills;
- Runbooks for critical operations;
- Source-code and artifact escrow where contractually required;
- Customer-specific customization inventory;
- Rotation of successor engineers through actual incidents.

Pay retention bonuses based partly on verified knowledge transfer, not solely time served. A successor team should demonstrate independent capability before key experts depart.

The company should also treat retiring engineers respectfully and avoid framing them as obsolete; they are essential to a safe transition.

---

# 9. Customer communication plan

## Communication principles

The message should be:

- Early;
- Unambiguous;
- Consistent across sales, support, product, and legal;
- Specific about dates and scope;
- Honest about feature gaps;
- Focused on customer continuity;
- Supported by practical resources.

Do not repeatedly extend dates after pressure from individual accounts. Each extension weakens credibility and encourages other customers to wait.

## Communication sequence

### Before announcement

- Brief employees, partners, and support teams;
- Prepare account-specific impact analyses;
- Contact the most strategic customers confidentially shortly before public communication where appropriate;
- Prepare FAQs, lifecycle documentation, commercial rules, and escalation paths.

### Formal announcement

State:

- Why the product is being retired;
- End-of-sale, standard-support, extended-support, and shutdown dates;
- What support is included at each stage;
- Replacement-product benefits;
- Feature disposition and roadmap;
- Migration options;
- Data export and retention policy;
- Contacts and next steps.

### Ongoing reminders

Provide notices at approximately:

- 24 months;
- 18 months;
- 12 months;
- 9 months;
- 6 months;
- 3 months;
- 30 days before each relevant support or shutdown milestone.

Use account communications, support portals, administrator messages, invoices, contract notices, and in-product banners as appropriate.

---

# 10. Governance and exception control

Create an executive steering committee led by a single accountable program executive and including:

- Product;
- Engineering;
- Security;
- Customer success;
- Sales;
- Services;
- Finance;
- Legal;
- Compliance;
- Operations;
- Communications.

## Core decision forums

### Capability council

Approves rebuild, partner, bridge, or retire decisions using one scorecard.

### Customer migration council

Reviews customer readiness, blocked migrations, red accounts, and commercial exceptions.

### Security and operational review

Determines whether the legacy platform remains safe to operate and whether risk requires acceleration.

### Financial review

Tracks profit transition, investment, incentive effectiveness, and avoided cost.

## Exception policy

Any extension beyond standard support should require:

- Documented business value;
- Security approval;
- Legal and contractual review;
- A funded migration plan;
- Customer milestones;
- Executive approval;
- An immutable expiration date.

No salesperson or account executive should be able to promise lifecycle extensions independently.

If a critical vulnerability cannot be remediated safely, security requirements override commercial timelines. Contracts and communications should reserve this right.

---

# 11. Program metrics and gates

## Migration and retention

- Percentage of customers assessed;
- Percentage with signed migration plans;
- Replacement contracts signed;
- Customers migrated by tier;
- Legacy annual recurring revenue converted;
- Gross profit converted;
- Retention and churn;
- Competitive losses;
- Expansion after migration;
- Time from assessment to go-live.

## Product readiness

- Must-rebuild features delivered and accepted;
- Number of migrations blocked by product gaps;
- Usage and adoption of rebuilt capabilities;
- Open critical defects;
- Workarounds and temporary bridges still active.

## Migration quality

- Migration duration and cost;
- Automation rate;
- Data-reconciliation success;
- Rollback frequency;
- Customer downtime;
- Severity-one incidents;
- 30-, 60-, and 90-day adoption;
- Customer satisfaction after migration.

## Legacy risk reduction

- Number of unsupported dependencies;
- Critical vulnerabilities;
- Legacy incidents;
- Unique-knowledge dependencies;
- Infrastructure cost;
- Custom forks eliminated;
- Percentage of successor engineers certified on runbooks;
- Data and customers remaining.

## Commercial health

- Incentive cost per retained dollar;
- Replacement contribution margin;
- Extended-support revenue and cost;
- Discount leakage;
- Lifetime value change;
- Revenue and profit at risk by migration wave.

## Program gates

Do not force a customer wave into production unless:

- Relevant must-have capabilities are available;
- Data reconciliation passes;
- Integrations have been tested;
- Security and compliance approvals are complete;
- Customer users are trained;
- Rollback or recovery is documented;
- Customer and company owners sign off.

Do not move the final sunset date merely because some customers deferred preparation. Use extended support and escalation to distinguish genuine blockers from inaction.

---

# 12. Key risks and mitigations

| Risk | Mitigation |
|---|---|
| Large customers use the sunset to run a competitive process | Engage early, prove replacement value, close critical gaps, and provide executive sponsorship |
| Company rebuilds the entire old product | Enforce capability scorecard and architectural approval |
| Customers wait until the final year | Deadline-based incentives, rising support fees, migration reservations, and repeated notices |
| Sales promises exceptions | Central approval and compensation tied to migrated revenue, not legacy renewal alone |
| Retiring engineers leave too early | Retention agreements, succession, documentation, and operational certification |
| Security incident accelerates shutdown | Isolation, patching, monitoring, contingency communications, and contractual emergency rights |
| Migration becomes excessively manual | Invest in migration tooling and standardized playbooks |
| New product loses focus | Dedicated gap team and strategic-fit requirements |
| Temporary compatibility becomes permanent | Explicit expiration dates and owners for every bridge |
| Profit declines faster than planned | Quarterly financial bridge, wave sequencing, expansion offers, and controlled investment |
| Customers lose historical access | Read-only archive, standardized exports, and documented retention options |
| Forced migration damages trust | Early transparency, evidence-based timelines, meaningful support, and honest gap management |

---

# Implementation priorities

## First 90 days

- Appoint the program executive and steering committee;
- complete the customer and contract inventory;
- quantify risk-adjusted legacy economics;
- inventory technical dependencies and knowledge concentration;
- identify the top niche-feature gaps;
- interview the highest-value and highest-risk customers;
- define lifecycle dates and exception rules;
- retain critical legacy engineers;
- begin migration-tool development;
- establish feature and customer scoring;
- prepare communications and commercial packages.

## Months 4–6

- Make rebuild, bridge, partner, and retirement decisions;
- commit replacement-roadmap capacity;
- launch Tier 1 design partnerships;
- finalize standard- and extended-support terms;
- test migration tooling on internal or friendly-customer datasets;
- train customer-facing teams;
- announce the lifecycle formally.

## Months 7–18

- Migrate low-complexity customers first;
- use early migrations to improve tools;
- conduct Tier 1 rehearsals;
- deliver must-have capabilities;
- publish references and migration evidence;
- remove unused infrastructure and custom forks progressively;
- escalate customers without plans.

## Months 19–30

- Complete most strategic and complex migrations;
- move laggards into formal executive escalation;
- restrict legacy changes further;
- prepare extended-support environments;
- validate archive and data-disposition procedures.

## Months 31–42, including extended support

- Operate only approved extended-support accounts;
- complete remaining migrations;
- stop temporary bridges;
- issue final notices;
- conduct shutdown rehearsals;
- terminate production services;
- certify data disposition;
- close infrastructure, vendor, and staffing obligations.

---

# Final recommendation

The company should announce a credible, approximately three-year end-of-life path with **standard support ending after two years and tightly restricted paid extended support ending one year later**. The date must be firm enough to change customer behavior but long enough to close genuine product gaps and migrate complex accounts safely.

The central choices are:

1. Rebuild niche capabilities only when they protect material value, serve critical workflows, fit the new architecture, and lack acceptable alternatives.
2. Use workflow redesign, APIs, partners, archives, and temporary bridges for specialized requirements.
3. Segment migration service by customer value and complexity.
4. Give strategic customers executive sponsorship, rehearsals, validated feature commitments, and limited commercial protection.
5. Give scaled customers automated tools, standard packages, and deadline-based incentives.
6. Price extended support to reflect its cost and risk, and make it conditional on a signed migration plan.
7. Capture legacy knowledge immediately and prove successor readiness.
8. Track converted profit, customer retention, security risk, and adoption—not merely the number of accounts technically moved.
9. Refuse indefinite exceptions and uneconomic custom rebuilds.
10. Make the replacement demonstrably better before asking the most vulnerable customers to commit.

A successful sunset converts the legacy product’s profit into durable replacement-platform value while reducing risk. An unsuccessful one either forces customers away before they are ready or preserves the old platform until security, staffing, and complexity make an orderly exit impossible.
